var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"title":"Modular API Reference","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Modular-API-Reference","category":"section","text":"To bridge the gap between modular software architecture and physical laboratory execution, this reference is organised into two complementary views:\n\nThe Experimental Pipeline (Chronological Workflow Map): A step-by-step guide matching standard laboratory protocol sequences.\nModular Architecture Reference: Complete function signatures, parameter descriptions, and docstrings grouped by library namespace (Lib_Core, Lib_Mole, Lib_Vise, Lib_Arts, Sys_Flow, Sys_Fast).\n\n"},{"title":"The Experimental Pipeline (Chronological Workflow Map)","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#The-Experimental-Pipeline-(Chronological-Workflow-Map)","category":"section","text":"  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ Stage 1: Formulation & Stoichiometric Audit      (Lib_Mole) │\n  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                                 │\n                                 ▼\n  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ Stage 2: Experimental Design & Optimality        (Lib_Core) │\n  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                                 │\n                                 ▼\n  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ Stage 3: Data Ingestion, Decay & Modelling       (Lib_Vise) │\n  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                                 │\n                                 ▼\n  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ Stage 4: Multi-Objective Optimisation & Pools    (Lib_Core) │\n  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                                 │\n                                 ▼\n  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ Stage 5: Interphase Knowledge Transfer (IPKT)    (Sys_Flow) │\n  │          [Phase 1 Leader  ──►  Phase 2 Search Bounds]       │\n  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                                 │\n                                 ▼\n  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │ Stage 6: Scientific Reporting & Visualisation    (Lib_Arts) │\n  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘"},{"title":"Stage 1: Formulation Specification & Stoichiometric Auditing","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Stage-1:-Formulation-Specification-and-Stoichiometric-Auditing","category":"section","text":"Pre-experimental verification of recipe mass balance, molar ratios, and concentration consistency.\n\nFunction Primary Role Laboratory Action\nMOLE_ParseTable_DDEF Component Parsing Extracts active reagents (VAR), fixed salts (FIX), and diluents (FILL) from input dictionaries or tables.\nMOLE_AuditQuick_DDEF Mass Conservation Audit Verifies that solute masses and diluent volumes sum strictly to the target concentration and total volume.\nMOLE_CalcMass_DDEF Mass Calculation Computes mass from volume, concentration, and molecular weight.\nMOLE_ProcessDesign_DDEF Protocol Synthesis Computes dynamic ingredient dispensing volumes for every single run across the experimental design matrix.\nMOLE_ValidatePhysicalUnit_DDEF Unit Verification Confirms validity and compatibility of physical laboratory units (e.g. mg, ug, mL, uL, mM).\n\n"},{"title":"Stage 2: Experimental Design Generation & Information Optimality","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Stage-2:-Experimental-Design-Generation-and-Information-Optimality","category":"section","text":"Constructing coded matrices, mapping physical laboratory units, and evaluating experimental efficiency.\n\nFunction Primary Role Laboratory Action\nCORE_GenDesign_DDEF Matrix Generator Generates 3-factor coded matrices for Box-Behnken (\"BB15\"), Central Composite (\"CD17\"), Taguchi (\"TL09\"), or Fractional D-Optimal (\"DF14\").\nCORE_CalcDesignMetrics_DDEF Optimality Diagnostics Evaluates D-, A-, G-, and I-efficiencies and condition numbers kappa(X^T X) for linear, interaction, or quadratic models.\nCORE_MapLevels_DDEF Coordinate Mapping Maps dimensionless coded levels -1 0 1 to physical equipment setpoints and experimental ranges.\nCORE_CodeMatrix_DDEF Level Normalisation Transforms raw physical laboratory values back to dimensionless coded coordinates for orthogonal regression.\nCORE_ValidateDesign_DDEF Geometry Validation Verifies matrix rank, balance, degree-of-freedom adequacy, and absence of degenerate rows.\n\n"},{"title":"Stage 3: Experimental Data Ingestion & Statistical Diagnostics","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Stage-3:-Experimental-Data-Ingestion-and-Statistical-Diagnostics","category":"section","text":"Correcting pre/post latency delays, fitting response surfaces, ANOVA, and cross-validation.\n\nFunction Primary Role Laboratory Action\nVISE_ApplyFRDecay_DDEF Latency Normalisation Corrects preparation delays (Delta t_textforw) and analytical delays (Delta t_textreve) using first-order rate kinetics.\nMOLE_CalcRadioDecay_DDEF Kinetic Decay Engine Computes forward degradation (exp(-lambda t)) or reverse reference restoration (exp(+lambda t)).\nVISE_Regress_DDEF Response Surface Fitter Fits Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) linear, 2-factor interaction, or full quadratic response surfaces.\nVISE_SelectBestModel_DDEF Parsimonious Model Selection Evaluates AICc, BIC, and adjusted R^2 to select the most robust model hierarchy without overfitting.\nVISE_GenANOVATable_DDEF ANOVA & Hypothesis Testing Computes Sequential and Adjusted Sum of Squares, mean squares, F-statistics, and p-values.\nVISE_TestLOF_DDEF Model Adequacy Diagnostic Partitions residual sum of squares into Pure Error (replicates) and Lack of Fit.\nVISE_CrossValidate_DDEF Predictive Power Diagnostic Evaluates Leave-One-Out cross-validated Q^2 and PRESS statistic to protect against model overparameterisation.\nVISE_CalcVIF_DDEF Multicollinearity Audit Computes Variance Inflation Factors (VIF) to detect collinear distortions among predictor terms.\n\n"},{"title":"Stage 4: Multi-Objective Optimisation & Candidate Pool Generation","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Stage-4:-Multi-Objective-Optimisation-and-Candidate-Pool-Generation","category":"section","text":"Arbitrating competing quality targets, applying kinetic trade-off penalties, and isolating leader portfolios.\n\nFunction Primary Role Laboratory Action\nCORE_OptimiseDesirability_DDEF Continuous Metaheuristic Solves multi-response Derringer-Suich desirability using BlackBoxOptim with optional DCYP kinetic penalty.\nVISE_RunGridSearch_DDEF Deterministic Grid Evaluation Evaluates regularised composite desirability across 9261 to 68921 nodes with multi-threaded parallel execution.\nCORE_ApplyDCYP_DDEF Kinetic Penalty Operator Penalises composite desirability D against duration: D_textadj = D cdot exp(-lambda Delta t).\nFLOW_GetCandidates_DDEF Candidate Pool Extraction Extracts Absolute Leaders (TOP-01, TOP-02), Input Minimisation (INP-<Factor>), and Output Specialisation (OUT-<Response>).\nFLOW_AuditLeader_DDEF Interactive Leader Selection Queries or selects a specific leader strategy from the candidate pool for interphase advancement.\n\n"},{"title":"Stage 5: Interphase Knowledge Transfer (Phase 1 → Phase 2 Transition)","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Stage-5:-Interphase-Knowledge-Transfer-(Phase-1-Phase-2-Transition)","category":"section","text":"Bridging sequential experimental phases, contracting search boundaries, and transferring historical variance.\n\nFunction Primary Role Laboratory Action\nFLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF Boundary Adaptation (ACTA) Computes contracted or translated factor boundaries L^textnew U^textnew around the selected leader.\nFLOW_ApplyACTA_DDEF Domain Contracting Operator Directly generates new continuous factor bounds using contraction ratio c and translation offset delta.\nFLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF Affine Mapping (ASTM) Scales and shifts physical factor boundaries (x = alpha x + beta) for equipment migration or volume scaling.\nFLOW_ValidateASTM_DDEF Absolute Boundary Clamping Enforces hard thermodynamic, physical, and safety boundaries L^textabs U^textabs onto adapted levels.\nFLOW_BuildIPKT_DDEF Master Transfer Protocol Builds the unified IPKT transition payload transferring active factors, fixed settings, and response targets from Phase 1 to Phase 2.\nFLOW_AdvancePhase_DDEF Phase Advancement Dispatch-driven progression from current experimental stage to subsequent phase (e.g. Phase 1 → Phase 2).\n\n"},{"title":"Stage 6: Scientific Reporting, Visualisation & Spreadsheet I/O","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Stage-6:-Scientific-Reporting,-Visualisation-and-Spreadsheet-I/O","category":"section","text":"Generating academic dossiers, interactive 3D response surfaces, and lossless Excel protocol sheets.\n\nFunction Primary Role Laboratory Action\nVISE_GenSciReport_DDEF Academic Dossier Generator Compiles a 7-stage interpreted scientific report ready for publication or regulatory filing.\nARTS_RenderSurface_DDEF 3D Response Surface Renders interactive Plotly 3D response surfaces with Viridis topography and optimal coordinate markers.\nARTS_RenderContour_DDEF 2D Equipotential Contours Plots 2D contour projections with fixed-factor slicing.\nARTS_RenderPareto_DDEF Pareto Effect Chart Displays standardised model coefficients sorted by statistical significance.\nARTS_RenderResiduals_DDEF Diagnostic Residual Plot Visualises studentised residuals versus fitted values to diagnose homoscedasticity.\nARTS_RenderFeasibleSpace_DDEF Feasible Space Projection Plots operational regions satisfying all individual response criteria (D ge 005).\nARTS_RenderOptimalZone_DDEF Optimal Zone Projection Plots near-optimal operating regions within 10% tolerance of summit desirability (D ge 090 times D_max).\nARTS_RenderSweetSpot_DDEF Sweet Spot Projection Plots primary operating cores within 5% tolerance of summit desirability (D ge 095 times D_max).\nFAST_WriteExcel_DDEF Protocol Export Writes multi-tab formatted Excel workbooks preserving cell types, styles, and formulas.\nFAST_ReadExcel_DDEF Protocol Ingestion Safely reads completed laboratory spreadsheets into DataFrames with strict type casting.\nFAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF Composite Plate Generator Assembles individual vector panels into standardised 145 mm multi-panel SVG plates.\n\n"},{"title":"Modular Architecture Reference","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Modular-Architecture-Reference","category":"section","text":"DoECISORY.jl provides both high-level and low-level APIs for programmatic execution. Currently, design serves as the primary high-level endpoint for experimental planning, with further high-level APIs planned for future releases. For direct low-level control, routines follow the symbol template:\n\n[DOMAIN]_[PascalCase-Action/Entity]_DDE[F|S|T|C]\n\nHere: [DOMAIN] identifies the subsystem, [PascalCase-Action/Entity] describes the operational action or data entity, DDE marks a DoECISORY-defined construct, and the terminal letter denotes functions (_DDEF), structs (_DDES), abstract types (_DDET), or constants (_DDEC) (e.g. VISE_AnalyseSensitivity_DDEF and CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES).\n\n"},{"title":"High-Level Public Entry Points","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#High-Level-Public-Entry-Points","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Experimental Design & Optimality (Lib_Core)","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Experimental-Design-and-Optimality-(Lib_Core)","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Stoichiometry & Mass Conservation (Lib_Mole)","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Stoichiometry-and-Mass-Conservation-(Lib_Mole)","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Statistical Modelling & ANOVA (Lib_Vise)","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Statistical-Modelling-and-ANOVA-(Lib_Vise)","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Response Surface Visualisation (Lib_Arts)","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Response-Surface-Visualisation-(Lib_Arts)","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Workflow Transitions (Sys_Flow)","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#Workflow-Transitions-(Sys_Flow)","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"File Exchange & System Utilities (Sys_Fast)","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#File-Exchange-and-System-Utilities-(Sys_Fast)","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"DoECISORY.launch","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.launch","category":"function","text":"launch(; host=\"0.0.0.0\", port=nothing, debug=false, open_browser=true, wait=false, start_server=true) -> Union{Nothing, Task, Dash.DashApp}\n\nLaunch the DoECISORY interactive web application in-process within the active Julia session. Initialises the experimental design formulation workspace (Deck) alongside the response surface analysis suite (Lens).\n\nKeywords\n\nhost::String: Host network interface address to bind (default: \"0.0.0.0\").\nport::Union{Int, Nothing}: Network port to bind (default: 8060 or 7860 on Hugging Face Spaces).\ndebug::Bool: Enables Dash developer hot-reloading and debug tools (default: false).\nopen_browser::Bool: Automatically launches the default web browser upon startup (default: true).\nwait::Bool: Blocks the calling process until the web server is terminated (default: false).\nstart_server::Bool: Whether to bind and launch the HTTP server (default: true). When false, returns the configured DashApp instance without opening sockets.\n\nReturns\n\nNothing when wait=true, the background server Task when wait=false, or DashApp when start_server=false.\n\nExamples\n\nusing DoECISORY\nlaunch()\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.design","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.design","category":"function","text":"design(method::Symbol=:bb15; factors=nothing, levels=nothing, limits=nothing, direction=[-1, -1, -1]) -> CALL_Design_DDES\n\nConstruct an experimental design plan for response surface methodology, screening, or directional optimization. Translates dimensionless coded coordinates into physical laboratory formulations via piecewise linear interpolation.\n\nArguments\n\nmethod::Symbol: Topology identifier (:bb15 for Box-Behnken, :cd17 for Central Composite, :tl09 for Taguchi L9, or :df14 for Directional Fractional CCD). Default is :bb15.\n\nKeywords\n\nfactors: Vector of factor names (default: [\"X1\", \"X2\", \"X3\"]).\nlevels: Vector of 3-element tuples (L1, L2, L3) representing lower (-1), centre (0), and upper (+1) physical levels for each factor.\nlimits: Optional vector of 2-element tuples (Min, Max) defining physical process operating constraints.\ndirection: Axial search vector for directional designs such as :df14 (default: [-1, -1, -1]).\n\nReturns\n\nCALL_Design_DDES: Structured design plan containing factor metadata, coded coordinates, real physical matrix, execution DataFrame, and optimality efficiency metrics.\n\nExamples\n\nusing DoECISORY\nplan = design(:bb15;\n    factors = [\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"],\n    levels  = [(60.0, 90.0, 120.0), (5.0, 15.0, 25.0), (15.0, 25.0, 35.0)]\n)\nshow(stdout, MIME(\"text/plain\"), plan.dataframe; allrows=true)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_GenDesign_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_GenDesign_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_GenDesign_DDEF(Method::AbstractString, FactorCount::Integer=3; Direction=[-1, -1, -1]) -> Matrix{Int8}\n\nGenerate a coded (-1, 0, 1) design matrix for a 3-factor response surface topology. Constructs standard Box-Behnken (BB15), Central Composite (CD17), Taguchi (TL09), or Directional Fractional (DF14) coordinate runs.\n\nArguments\n\nMethod::AbstractString: Design identifier (\"BB15\", \"CD17\", \"TL09\", or \"DF14\").\nFactorCount::Integer: Number of continuous factors (fixed at 3).\n\nKeywords\n\nDirection::AbstractVector: Search orientation vector for \"DF14\" design (default: [-1, -1, -1]).\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix{Int8}: Coded coordinate matrix of size N × 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_BuildMatrix_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_BuildMatrix_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_BuildMatrix_DDEF(Method, fc=3, dir=[-1, -1, -1]) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nGenerate the normalised design matrix for the specified experimental method dispatch type or string identifier. Validates the factor count requirement and dispatches to the corresponding topology constructor.\n\nArguments\n\nMethod: Design method object or string identifier (\"TL09\", \"BB15\", \"CD17\", \"DF14\").\nfc: Factor count (must be 3 for standard response surface topologies).\ndir: Search direction vector for directional designs (e.g. DF14).\n\nReturns\n\nA Matrix{Float64} containing normalised coordinate points in the domain [-1, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_MapLevels_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_MapLevels_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_MapLevels_DDEF(CodedMatrix::AbstractMatrix, Config::AbstractVector) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nMap coded coordinates in [-1.0, 1.0] to physical engineering units via piecewise linear interpolation. Interpolates discrete level boundaries to translate dimensionless designs into actual laboratory formulations.\n\nArguments\n\nCodedMatrix::AbstractMatrix: Matrix of coded coordinates (N × 3).\nConfig::AbstractVector: Factor configurations containing lower (L1), centre (L2), and upper (L3) physical levels.\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix{Float64}: Interpolated physical values of size N × 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CodeMatrix_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CodeMatrix_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_CodeMatrix_DDEF(X::AbstractMatrix, Bounds::Union{AbstractMatrix, Nothing}=nothing) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nTransform physical factor values into dimensionless coded coordinates in the standard range [-1.0, 1.0]. Enables scale-invariant model fitting and condition number evaluation across factors with differing physical dimensions.\n\nArguments\n\nX::AbstractMatrix: Matrix of physical experimental factor coordinates (N × k).\nBounds::Union{AbstractMatrix, Nothing}: Optional explicit k × 2 matrix of [min, max] domain bounds.\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix{Float64}: Dimensionless coded matrix with coordinates scaled to [-1.0, 1.0].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ExpandModelMatrix_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ExpandModelMatrix_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_ExpandModelMatrix_DDEF(X::AbstractMatrix, ModelType=CORE_ModelQuadratic_DDES()) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nExpand a 3-factor coded matrix into linear (p=4) or full quadratic (p=10) model design columns. Generates intercept, linear terms, cross-product two-way interactions, and pure quadratic squared columns.\n\nArguments\n\nX::AbstractMatrix: Coded factor matrix of size N × 3.\nModelType: Model type object (CORE_ModelLinear_DDES, CORE_ModelQuadratic_DDES) or name string (\"linear\", \"quadratic\").\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix{Float64}: Expanded model matrix of size N × 4 (linear) or N × 10 (intercept, linear, interaction, quadratic).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CalcDEfficiency_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CalcDEfficiency_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_CalcDEfficiency_DDEF(X::AbstractMatrix, ModelType=CORE_ModelQuadratic_DDES()) -> Float64\n\nCalculate the normalised D-optimality efficiency criterion based on the Fisher information matrix determinant. Quantifies the volume of the joint confidence ellipsoid for parameter estimates relative to an ideal orthogonal design.\n\nArguments\n\nX::AbstractMatrix: Experimental factor coordinates (N × 3).\nModelType: Model specification type or name string (\"linear\" or \"quadratic\").\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: D-efficiency metric (|X'X| / N^p)^(1/p) in [0.0, 1.0].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CalcDesignMetrics_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CalcDesignMetrics_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_CalcDesignMetrics_DDEF(X::AbstractMatrix, ModelType=CORE_ModelQuadratic_DDES()) -> Dict{String, Float64}\n\nCompute information matrix conditioning and optimality criteria (D-, A-, G-, and I-efficiencies). Provides a comprehensive diagnostic assessment of parameter variance, prediction error dispersion, and numerical stability.\n\nArguments\n\nX::AbstractMatrix: Experimental factor coordinates (N × 3).\nModelType: Model specification type or name string (\"linear\" or \"quadratic\").\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Float64}: Dictionary containing \"D\", \"A\", \"G\", \"I\" efficiency scores and matrix \"Condition\".\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CalcCriterion_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CalcCriterion_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_CalcCriterion_DDEF(Criterion::CORE_AbstractCriterion_DDET, arg, p::Int, R::Int) -> Float64\nCORE_CalcCriterion_DDEF(Criterion::AbstractString, arg, p::Int, R::Int) -> Float64\n\nCalculate information matrix optimality efficiency criteria (D, A, G, or I) using multiple dispatch. Evaluates parameter estimation precision, average variance, and prediction dispersion across experimental designs.\n\nArguments\n\nCriterion: Optimality criterion trait (CORE_CriterionD_DDES(), CORE_CriterionA_DDES(), CORE_CriterionG_DDES(), CORE_CriterionI_DDES()) or string identifier (\"D\", \"A\", \"G\", \"I\").\narg: Information matrix X^T X, its inverse (X^T X)^-1, or leverage diagonal vector h = operatornamediag(X (X^T X)^-1 X^T).\np::Int: Number of estimated model parameters.\nR::Int: Number of experimental design runs.\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Normalised efficiency score bounded in 00 10.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_OptimiseDesirability_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_OptimiseDesirability_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_OptimiseDesirability_DDEF(Models::AbstractVector, Goals::AbstractVector, X_Bounds::AbstractMatrix{Float64}; kwargs...) -> Tuple{Vector{Float64}, Float64}\n\nLocate optimal continuous factor coordinates by maximising composite multi-response desirability via global metaheuristics. Evaluates geometric-mean composite objective functions across all active response models within the designated bounding box.\n\nArguments\n\nModels::AbstractVector: Fitted regression models for all active responses.\nGoals::AbstractVector: Optimisation goals and target specifications per response.\nX_Bounds::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Lower and upper search domain bounds of size 3 × 2.\n\nKeywords\n\nMaxTime::Float64: Maximum optimisation time budget in seconds (default: 1.0).\nPenaltyFn::Union{Function,Nothing}: Optional boundary constraint penalty function.\nModifiersDCYP::Vector{CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES}: Optional radiochemical decay penalty modifiers.\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Vector{Float64}, Float64}: (best_coordinates, best_composite_desirability).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CalcDesirability_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_CalcDesirability_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_CalcDesirability_DDEF(Val::Float64, Goal) -> Float64\n\nCalculate the Derringer-Suich individual desirability score in [0.0, 1.0] for an evaluated response. Applies one-sided or two-sided piecewise exponential utility curves based on the assigned optimisation goal.\n\nArguments\n\nVal::Float64: Evaluated or predicted response value.\nGoal: Goal parameter dictionary or extracted Tuple containing type, limits, target, and exponential weight.\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Normalised desirability index clamped between 0.0 (unacceptable) and 1.0 (ideal).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ExtractGoal_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ExtractGoal_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_ExtractGoal_DDEF(Goal::AbstractDict) -> Tuple{CORE_AbstractGoalType_DDET, Float64, Float64, Float64, Float64}\n\nParse and validate response desirability goal parameters into a typed dispatch tuple. Extracts numerical limits, target thresholds, and exponential importance weights while resolving default midpoints.\n\nArguments\n\nGoal::AbstractDict: Dictionary containing \"Type\" (\"Max\", \"Min\", or \"Nominal\"), \"Min\", \"Max\", \"Target\", and \"Weight\".\n\nReturns\n\nTuple: (Trait, G_Min, G_Max, G_Tgt, Weight) where Trait is a CORE_AbstractGoalType_DDET.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ValidateDesign_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ValidateDesign_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_ValidateDesign_DDEF(DesignMatrix::AbstractMatrix, Config::AbstractVector=[]) -> Tuple{Bool, String}\n\nPerform pre-flight numerical and structural validation on an experimental design matrix. Checks for minimum sample size, zero-variance columns, excessive duplicate runs, and matrix near-singularity.\n\nArguments\n\nDesignMatrix::AbstractMatrix: Matrix of candidate experimental runs (N × k).\nConfig::AbstractVector: Optional factor configurations.\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Bool, String}: (is_valid, report) indicating rank sufficiency, column variance, and duplicate runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ExtractLeader_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ExtractLeader_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_ExtractLeader_DDEF(FilePath::AbstractString, PhaseCode::AbstractString, SelectedID::AbstractString=\"\", VarNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}=String[]) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nExtract the optimal leader run coordinates and desirability score from an experimental phase record. Reads the specified phase candidate table, retrieves the global best or user-selected run, and extracts factor levels.\n\nArguments\n\nFilePath::AbstractString: Path to the master Excel workbook.\nPhaseCode::AbstractString: Phase identifier (e.g. \"Phase1\").\nSelectedID::AbstractString: Optional specific run identifier (defaults to highest score if empty).\nVarNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Specific factor names to extract.\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Dictionary containing \"ID\", \"Score\", \"Vals\" (factor coordinates), and \"InputNames\".\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES","category":"type","text":"CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES(TimeIndex::Int, Lambda::Float64, IsotopeName::String)\n\nDecay-Corrected Yield Penalty structure for radiochemical multi-objective optimisation. Stores the reaction time column index and decay constant to penalise desirability as physical time elapses.\n\nFields\n\nTimeIndex::Int: Column index of the incubation or reaction duration factor.\nLambda::Float64: Radioactive decay constant in reciprocal time units (ln(2) / half_life).\nIsotopeName::String: Radionuclide identifier for tracking and reporting.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ApplyDCYP_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Core.CORE_ApplyDCYP_DDEF","category":"function","text":"CORE_ApplyDCYP_DDEF(Score::Float64, Mod::CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES, x) -> Float64\n\nApply an exponential radio-decay penalty factor exp(-lambda * t) to composite desirability. Resolves the trade-off between chemical reaction completion and radioactive decay loss during formulation.\n\nArguments\n\nScore::Float64: Raw composite desirability score in [0.0, 1.0].\nMod::CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES: Decay modifier specifying time factor index and decay constant.\nx: Candidate factor coordinate vector containing the reaction duration.\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Decay-penalised desirability score in [0.0, 1.0].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ParseTable_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ParseTable_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_ParseTable_DDEF(TableData::AbstractVector) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nParse tabular formulation records from the UI DataTable into categorized operational arrays. Extracts component identifiers, functional roles, discrete level bounds, and molecular weights into indexed dictionaries.\n\nArguments\n\nTableData::AbstractVector: Array of row dictionaries containing formulation parameters.\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Dictionary containing \"Names\", \"Roles\", \"Mins\", \"Mids\", \"Maxs\", and \"MWs\".\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_AuditQuick_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_AuditQuick_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_AuditQuick_DDEF(TableData::AbstractVector, Vol::Float64, Conc::Float64) -> Tuple{Bool, String, DataFrame, Float64, Dict}\n\nExecute a rapid gravimetric and stoichiometric audit on baseline formulation recipes. Verifies variable factor counts, checks mass conservation limits, and automatically computes filler reagent quantities.\n\nArguments\n\nTableData::AbstractVector: Vector of formulation component dictionaries.\nVol::Float64: Target batch volume in millilitres.\nConc::Float64: Target overall molar concentration in millimoles per litre.\n\nReturns\n\nTuple: (is_valid, audit_report_text, summary_dataframe, total_batch_mass_mg, filler_info_dict).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_CalcMass_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_CalcMass_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_CalcMass_DDEF(Names::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, MWs::AbstractVector{Float64}, Ratios::AbstractVector{Float64}, Tgt_Vol::Float64, Tgt_Conc::Float64, Units::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, Scale::Float64=1.0; kwargs...) -> DataFrame\n\nResolve multi-component gravimetric mass requirements using a multi-pass stoichiometric reconciliation engine. Processes absolute mass and concentration constraints first, allocates internal percentages, and distributes residual moles across stoichiometric components.\n\nArguments\n\nNames: Vector of ingredient names.\nMWs: Molecular weights in g/mol.\nRatios: Component formulation ratios or level values.\nTgt_Vol: Total batch volume in millilitres.\nTgt_Conc: Target total concentration in millimolar.\nUnits: Component physical unit strings.\nScale: Optional scaling factor (default: 1.0).\n\nKeywords\n\nSuppressLog::Bool: Mutes warning logs during iterative solver loops (default: false).\n\nReturns\n\nDataFrame: Table containing resolved masses (TARGET_MASS_mg), millimoles (Moles_mmol), and normalised percentages.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ApproxEq_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ApproxEq_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_ApproxEq_DDEF(a::Real, b::Real; atol::Float64=1e-6) -> Bool\n\nEvaluate floating-point equality between chemical quantities within a predefined absolute tolerance. Prevents false-negative validation failures arising from floating-point rounding during mass balance checks.\n\nArguments\n\na::Real: First numeric quantity.\nb::Real: Second numeric quantity.\n\nKeywords\n\natol::Float64: Absolute comparison tolerance (default: 1e-6).\n\nReturns\n\nBool: true if absolute difference |a - b| <= atol, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ValidatePhysicalUnit_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ValidatePhysicalUnit_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_ValidatePhysicalUnit_DDEF(ValueStr::AbstractString, ExpectedType::AbstractString) -> Tuple{Bool, Float64, String}\n\nValidate physical dimensional correctness using strict Unitful dimensional analysis. Ensures entered values match expected physical dimensions (Volume, Concentration, Mass, or Time) and converts them to base units.\n\nArguments\n\nValueStr::AbstractString: Input string containing numeric magnitude and unit (e.g. \"250 uL\").\nExpectedType::AbstractString: Expected physical dimension (\"Volume\", \"Concentration\", \"Mass\", \"Time\", or \"Ratio\").\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Bool, Float64, String}: (is_valid, magnitude_in_system_units, status_message).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_AuditMatrix_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_AuditMatrix_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_AuditMatrix_DDEF(Design::AbstractMatrix, Names::AbstractVector, MWs::AbstractVector, Vol::Float64, Conc::Float64, Units::AbstractVector=fill(\"-\", length(Names))) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute total gravimetric mass requirements for every individual run in an experimental design matrix. Iterates across all design coordinate rows to evaluate run-by-run batch masses and detect gravimetric inconsistencies.\n\nArguments\n\nDesign::AbstractMatrix: Matrix of experimental runs (N × k).\nNames::AbstractVector: Ingredient identifiers.\nMWs::AbstractVector: Molecular weights in g/mol.\nVol::Float64: Target batch volume in millilitres.\nConc::Float64: Target concentration in millimolar.\nUnits::AbstractVector: Physical unit strings for each ingredient.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: Array of total batch masses in milligrams for each experimental run.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_AuditBatch_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_AuditBatch_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_AuditBatch_DDEF(TableData::AbstractVector, Design::AbstractMatrix, Vol::Float64, Conc::Float64) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nConduct a batch-wide stoichiometric feasibility assessment for an experimental design matrix. Computes mass statistics across all candidate runs, identifies negative component quantities, and verifies budget feasibility.\n\nArguments\n\nTableData::AbstractVector: Formulation table metadata.\nDesign::AbstractMatrix: Coded or physical experimental design matrix.\nVol::Float64: Batch volume in millilitres.\nConc::Float64: Target batch concentration in millimolar.\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Dictionary containing \"IsFeasible\", \"AvgMass_mg\", \"MaxMass_mg\", \"MinMass_mg\", \"StdDev_mg\", and \"RunMasses\".\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ValidateFeasibility_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ValidateFeasibility_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_ValidateFeasibility_DDEF(DesignMatrix::AbstractMatrix, InMeta::AbstractVector, Vol::Float64=100.0, Conc::Float64=10.0) -> Tuple{Bool, String}\n\nValidate stoichiometric mass balance feasibility across all coordinate points of an experimental design. Checks every design run for molar budget overconsumption and verifies that continuous factor levels do not generate negative quantities.\n\nArguments\n\nDesignMatrix::AbstractMatrix: Physical experimental factor coordinates (N × 3).\nInMeta::AbstractVector: Ingredient metadata dictionaries with roles, units, and molecular weights.\nVol::Float64: Batch volume in millilitres (default: 100.0).\nConc::Float64: Target concentration in millimolar (default: 10.0).\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Bool, String}: (is_valid, validation_report) detailing any violating run indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_CalcRadioDecay_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_CalcRadioDecay_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_CalcRadioDecay_DDEF(RawValue::Real, HalfLife::Real, HalfLifeUnit::AbstractString, DeltaTMinutes::Real; Reverse::Bool=false) -> Float64\n\nCalculate decayed activity or mass using first-order radioactive kinetics N(t) = N_0 * exp(±lambda * t). Supports forward decay compensation and backward initial-activity reconstruction for short-lived radiotracers.\n\nArguments\n\nRawValue::Real: Initial radioactivity or compound quantity.\nHalfLife::Real: Radionuclide half-life value.\nHalfLifeUnit::AbstractString: Half-life time unit (e.g. \"min\", \"h\", \"days\").\nDeltaTMinutes::Real: Elapsed time interval in minutes.\n\nKeywords\n\nReverse::Bool: If true, computes pre-decay activity N_0 = N(t) * exp(+lambda * t) (default: false).\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Decay-corrected quantity.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ProcessDesign_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ProcessDesign_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_ProcessDesign_DDEF(DesignMatrix::AbstractMatrix, TableData::AbstractVector, Vol::Float64, Conc::Float64) -> DataFrame\n\nTranslate a design matrix into a fully specified laboratory protocol worksheet with mass balance calculations. Constructs standardised input, fixed, and filler column headers while resolving run-by-run masses and residual volumes.\n\nArguments\n\nDesignMatrix::AbstractMatrix: Physical coordinate matrix (N × 3).\nTableData::AbstractVector: Formulation component rows.\nVol::Float64: Total formulation volume in millilitres.\nConc::Float64: Total concentration in millimoles per litre.\n\nReturns\n\nDataFrame: Complete experimental protocol table ready for execution and Excel workbook export.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_GetPercentageEquivalent_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_GetPercentageEquivalent_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_GetPercentageEquivalent_DDEF(Value::Float64, UnitStr::AbstractString, MW::Float64, Vol::Float64, Conc::Float64) -> Float64\n\nCalculate the equivalent molar percentage contribution of a formulation component within the total system budget. Translates absolute masses, molar concentrations, and percentage ratios into a unified percentage scale for mass audit.\n\nArguments\n\nValue::Float64: Ingredient level value.\nUnitStr::AbstractString: Physical unit string (e.g. \"mg\", \"mM\", \"%\").\nMW::Float64: Molecular weight in grams per mole.\nVol::Float64: Total formulation volume in millilitres.\nConc::Float64: Target overall molar concentration in millimoles per litre.\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Equivalent molar percentage contribution in [0.0, 100.0].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_IsTimeUnit_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_IsTimeUnit_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_IsTimeUnit_DDEF(UnitStr::AbstractString) -> Bool\n\nDetermine whether a given physical unit string represents a temporal dimension. Enables the Decay-Coupled Yield Penalty engine to automatically detect incubation time variables among formulation factors.\n\nArguments\n\nUnitStr::AbstractString: Unit string to evaluate.\n\nReturns\n\nBool: true if unit matches recognised time dimensions, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ConvertToMinutes_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Mole.MOLE_ConvertToMinutes_DDEF","category":"function","text":"MOLE_ConvertToMinutes_DDEF(Value::Real, Unit::AbstractString) -> Float64\n\nNormalise arbitrary time duration units (seconds, hours, days) into minutes. Provides dimensional time standardisation across kinetic calculations and decay compensation models.\n\nArguments\n\nValue::Real: Numeric time duration value.\nUnit::AbstractString: Time unit string (\"s\", \"sec\", \"min\", \"h\", \"hr\", \"d\").\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Duration converted into decimal minutes.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_Regress_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_Regress_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_Regress_DDEF(X_Raw::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Y::AbstractVector{Float64}, ModelType::AbstractString; InNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}=String[]) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nFit an ordinary least squares (OLS) regression model with comprehensive diagnostic telemetry. Computes parameter estimates via QR decomposition alongside leverage, Student's t-statistics, variance inflation, and condition numbers.\n\nArguments\n\nX_Raw::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental design factor matrix (N × k).\nY::AbstractVector{Float64}: Observed experimental response vector (N).\nModelType::AbstractString: Model structure (\"linear\" or \"quadratic\").\n\nKeywords\n\nInNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Optional factor labels for parameter naming (default: String[]).\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Model dictionary containing coefficients, goodness-of-fit metrics, and diagnostic indicators.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_SelectBestModel_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_SelectBestModel_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_SelectBestModel_DDEF(X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Y::AbstractVector{Float64}, InNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, RequestedType::AbstractString=\"Auto\") -> Tuple{Dict{String, Any}, String}\n\nEvaluate candidate model architectures and select the optimal regression structure based on predictive validity. Compares linear and quadratic formulations using cross-validated Q² and adjusted R² while preventing over-parameterisation.\n\nArguments\n\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Factor coordinate matrix (N × k).\nY::AbstractVector{Float64}: Observed experimental response vector (N).\nInNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor names.\nRequestedType::AbstractString: Architectural directive (\"Auto\", \"linear\", or \"quadratic\").\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Dict{String, Any}, String}: (BestModelDictionary, SelectionAuditMessage).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_Predict_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_Predict_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_Predict_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, X_Raw::Any) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute response predictions for candidate factor coordinates using fitted regression coefficients. Expands input coordinates to match the underlying model topology and computes matrix-vector inner products.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted regression model dictionary containing coefficients and model type.\nX_Raw::Any: Coordinate matrix or vector of evaluation points.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: Model-predicted response values.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_CrossValidate_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_CrossValidate_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_CrossValidate_DDEF(X_Raw::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Y::AbstractVector{Float64}, ModelType::AbstractString) -> Float64\n\nCompute leave-one-out cross-validated coefficient of determination (Q²) via the prediction error sum of squares (PRESS). Utilises the hat matrix diagonal shortcut to evaluate out-of-sample predictive performance without iterative re-fitting.\n\nArguments\n\nX_Raw::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Factor coordinate matrix.\nY::AbstractVector{Float64}: Observed response vector.\nModelType::AbstractString: Model structure (\"linear\" or \"quadratic\").\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Cross-validation metric Q² in (-Inf, 1.0].\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_CalcMetrics_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_CalcMetrics_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_CalcMetrics_DDEF(Y_Real::AbstractVector{Float64}, Y_Pred::AbstractVector{Float64}, p::Int) -> Tuple{Float64, Float64, Float64, Float64}\n\nCalculate fundamental goodness-of-fit and information theoretic metrics for regression validation. Computes raw coefficient of determination (R²), adjusted R², root mean square error (RMSE), and Akaike Information Criterion (AIC).\n\nArguments\n\nY_Real::AbstractVector{Float64}: Observed response measurements.\nY_Pred::AbstractVector{Float64}: Model-predicted response values.\np::Int: Number of estimated parameters in the regression model.\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Float64, Float64, Float64, Float64}: (R2, R2_Adj, RMSE, AIC).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_GenANOVATable_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_GenANOVATable_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_GenANOVATable_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, X_Raw::Any, Y_Raw::Any) -> DataFrame\n\nConstruct an analysis of variance (ANOVA) table partitioning total response variance into regression and residual components. Computes degrees of freedom, sums of squares, mean squares, F-statistics, and p-values for model and lack-of-fit terms.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted regression model dictionary.\nX_Raw::Any: Experimental factor design coordinates.\nY_Raw::Any: Observed response observations.\n\nReturns\n\nDataFrame: Standardised ANOVA summary table.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_TestLOF_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_TestLOF_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_TestLOF_DDEF(X_Design::AbstractMatrix, Y::AbstractVector) -> Tuple{Float64, Float64}\n\nPerform a formal Lack-of-Fit F-test to assess whether regression residual error exceeds experimental pure error. Partitions residual variance by grouping replicate design coordinates to evaluate model structural adequacy.\n\nArguments\n\nX_Design::AbstractMatrix: Expanded model design matrix.\nY::AbstractVector: Observed experimental responses.\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Float64, Float64}: (F_Statistic, P_Value) where NaN indicates absence of coordinate replicates.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_CalcVIF_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_CalcVIF_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_CalcVIF_DDEF(X_Design::AbstractMatrix) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCalculate Variance Inflation Factors (VIF) to detect multicollinearity among regression terms. Inverts the correlation matrix of predictor columns with Tikhonov regularisation for ill-conditioned designs.\n\nArguments\n\nX_Design::AbstractMatrix: Expanded model design matrix including intercept.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: Variance inflation factors corresponding to each model parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_TestNormality_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_TestNormality_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_TestNormality_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, X_Raw::Any, Y_Raw::Any) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nConduct a Shapiro-Wilk statistical hypothesis test on model residuals to verify Gaussian distribution assumptions. Calculates residual deviations between observed responses and model predictions before computing the test p-value.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted regression model dictionary.\nX_Raw::Any: Factor coordinate data.\nY_Raw::Any: Observed response data.\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Dictionary containing the p-value, normality conclusion flag, and test identifier.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_RunGridSearch_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_RunGridSearch_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_RunGridSearch_DDEF(Models::AbstractVector, Goals::AbstractVector, X_Bounds::AbstractMatrix{Float64}; Steps::Int=41, ModifiersDCYP::Vector{CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES}=CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES[]) -> Tuple{Matrix{Float64}, Matrix{Float64}, Vector{Float64}}\n\nExecute a high-density grid evaluation across continuous factor bounds to explore multi-response desirability landscapes. Evaluates multi-model predictions and computes composite desirability scores with optional radiochemical decay penalties.\n\nArguments\n\nModels::AbstractVector: Fitted regression models for each response variable.\nGoals::AbstractVector: Target criteria and desirability specifications.\nX_Bounds::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Lower and upper coordinate limits for each factor (k × 2).\n\nKeywords\n\nSteps::Int: Discretisation density per factor dimension (default: 41 or 21 depending on CPU threads).\nModifiersDCYP::Vector{CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES}: Optional radiochemical decay modifiers.\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Matrix{Float64}, Matrix{Float64}, Vector{Float64}}: (CandidateCoordinates, PredictionsMatrix, DesirabilityScores).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_AnalyseSensitivity_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_AnalyseSensitivity_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_AnalyseSensitivity_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, X_Point::AbstractVector{Float64}, X_Clean::AbstractMatrix{Float64}) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute normalised local sensitivity gradients around an evaluation coordinate point. Evaluates finite-difference numerical partial derivatives scaled by factor domain spans to determine fractional contributions.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted regression model dictionary.\nX_Point::AbstractVector{Float64}: Evaluation coordinate point (k factors).\nX_Clean::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental design coordinates used to resolve factor domain ranges.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: Normalised fractional sensitivities summing to 1.0.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_GenSciReport_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_GenSciReport_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_GenSciReport_DDEF(Res::AbstractDict) -> String\n\nGenerate an academic Markdown audit report detailing statistical findings and experimental design metrics. Compiles design vitals, regression equations, cross-validation metrics, ANOVA tables, and multi-response optimisation summaries.\n\nArguments\n\nRes::AbstractDict: Results dictionary produced by VISE_Execute_DDEF.\n\nReturns\n\nString: Formatted publication-grade Markdown document.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_ExportToExcel_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_ExportToExcel_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_ExportToExcel_DDEF(Res::AbstractDict, FilePath::AbstractString) -> Bool\n\nExport statistical results, model coefficients, and candidate leader formulations into a formatted multi-sheet Excel workbook. Generates structured worksheets for executive summaries, ANOVA tables, parameter estimates, and optimisation rankings.\n\nArguments\n\nRes::AbstractDict: Analytical results dictionary produced by VISE_Execute_DDEF.\nFilePath::AbstractString: Destination path for the exported workbook.\n\nReturns\n\nBool: true if export succeeded, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_ApplyFRDecay_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_ApplyFRDecay_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_ApplyFRDecay_DDEF(X, Y, in_n, out_n, df, config, opts, C, Log; mask=trues(nrow(df))) -> Vector{Dict{String, Any}}\n\nApply forward precursor decay and reverse product decay corrections across experimental data matrices. Adjusts precursor masses for elapsed synthesis duration and reconstructs delayed product activity back to end of synthesis.\n\nArguments\n\nX: Input factor coordinate matrix.\nY: Output response measurement matrix.\nin_n: Factor component names.\nout_n: Response variable names.\ndf: Experimental data table.\nconfig: Formulation configuration dictionary.\nopts: Analysis options dictionary.\nC: System constants instance (FAST_Data_DDEC).\nLog: Logging function dispatch.\n\nKeywords\n\nmask: Row selection bitmask for filtering valid experimental runs (default: all rows).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Dict{String, Any}}: Audit records detailing decay corrections and applied decay factors.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_ExtractDCYP_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_ExtractDCYP_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_ExtractDCYP_DDEF(in_n::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, out_n::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, config::AbstractDict, opts::AbstractDict) -> Vector{CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES}\n\nExtract Decay-Coupled Yield Penalty (DCYP) modifier structures from configuration and runtime options. Identifies reaction incubation time factors and resolves radionuclide half-lives to penalise multi-response desirability.\n\nArguments\n\nin_n::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor component names.\nout_n::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Response metric names.\nconfig::AbstractDict: Formulation configuration dictionary.\nopts::AbstractDict: Analytical execution options.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES}: Decay modifier parameter structures for desirability penalisation.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_Execute_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_Execute_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_Execute_DDEF(DataFile::AbstractString, Phase::AbstractString, Goals::AbstractVector, ModelType::AbstractString=\"Auto\"; kwargs...) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nExecute the complete statistical modelling and multi-objective optimisation pipeline for a designated experimental phase. Coordinates data ingestion, radiochemical decay compensation, ensemble model fitting, ANOVA diagnostics, and desirability optimisation.\n\nArguments\n\nDataFile::AbstractString: Path to the Excel workbook containing experimental data.\nPhase::AbstractString: Target experimental phase identifier (e.g. \"Phase1\").\nGoals::AbstractVector: Vector of response optimisation criteria.\nModelType::AbstractString: Model selection strategy (\"Auto\", \"linear\", or \"quadratic\").\n\nKeywords\n\nOpts::AbstractDict: Optimisation and radio-decay configuration options (default: empty dictionary).\nConfigUpdates::AbstractDict: Configuration metadata overrides (default: empty dictionary).\nt_start::Float64: Execution start timestamp for pipeline telemetry (default: time()).\nRenderMode::Symbol: Visualisation level (:Full or :Minimal).\nOptim::Bool: Whether to execute numerical desirability optimisation (default: true).\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Comprehensive analytical results dictionary.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_GetTermNames_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_GetTermNames_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_GetTermNames_DDEF(InNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, ModelType::Any) -> Vector{String}\n\nGenerate standardised labels for regression model terms including linear, interaction, and quadratic components. Formats polynomial combinations using mathematical unicode symbols according to the chosen model specification.\n\nArguments\n\nInNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Experimental factor labels.\nModelType::Any: Model specification (\"linear\" or \"quadratic\").\n\nReturns\n\nVector{String}: Ordered list of regression parameter names.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_ExpandDesign_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Vise.VISE_ExpandDesign_DDEF","category":"function","text":"VISE_ExpandDesign_DDEF(X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, ModelType::AbstractString) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nExpand continuous factor coordinates into a full polynomial design matrix for regression estimation. Constructs column blocks for the unit intercept, linear terms, two-factor interaction cross-products, and squared quadratics.\n\nArguments\n\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Raw factor coordinates of size N × k.\nModelType::AbstractString: Expansion topology (\"linear\" or \"quadratic\").\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix{Float64}: Expanded model matrix of size N × p.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderSurface_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderSurface_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderSurface_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Idx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Lbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender an interactive 3D response surface mesh illustrating response behaviour across two active factors. Holds unselected factors constant at their domain midpoint while evaluating curvature using continuous Viridis colours.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted response surface regression model dictionary.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental factor coordinate matrix.\nIdx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}: Pair of column indices for the x and y axes.\nLbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Human-readable factor labels for axis annotation.\nOutName::AbstractString: Name of the plotted response variable.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Interactive 3D Plotly surface visualization.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderContour_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderContour_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderContour_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Idx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Lbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender a 2D contour projection index representing response surface topography across two factors. Computes iso-response contour curves over an interpolated grid using the academic Viridis colour palette.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted regression model dictionary.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Factor coordinate matrix.\nIdx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}: Indices of the two continuous factors to project.\nLbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Axis labels for the selected factors.\nOutName::AbstractString: Plotted response variable name.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Plotly 2D contour plot with labelled iso-lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderSlice_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderSlice_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderSlice_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Idx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Lbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender a 2D interaction slice diagram demonstrating two-factor interaction effects on a target response. Plots the response curve across factor 1 at the minimum, mean, and maximum settings of factor 2.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted regression model dictionary.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental design factor matrix.\nIdx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}: Column indices of primary and moderating factors.\nLbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Descriptive labels for the evaluated factor pair.\nOutName::AbstractString: Response variable name.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Multi-trace interaction plot showing conditional trajectories.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderTrend_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderTrend_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderTrend_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Y_Real::AbstractVector{Float64}, Idx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Lbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender a main effect trend line showing predicted response variation across a single factor dimension. Superimposes experimental observations onto the model estimation curve while holding remaining factors at their centres.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted regression model dictionary.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental factor coordinate matrix.\nY_Real::AbstractVector{Float64}: Observed experimental response data.\nIdx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}: Single-element vector or index specifying the active factor.\nLbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor label for x-axis annotation.\nOutName::AbstractString: Response variable name.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Main effect plot with overlaid experimental observations.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderPareto_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderPareto_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderPareto_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, OutName::AbstractString, R2_Adj::AbstractFloat, R2_Pred::AbstractFloat) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender a horizontal Pareto bar chart ranking standardised regression factor effects by absolute t-value magnitude. Includes a Bonferroni-corrected critical significance threshold line to highlight statistically influential experimental factors.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted regression model dictionary containing coefficients and term labels.\nOutName::AbstractString: Response variable name for chart titling.\nR2_Adj::AbstractFloat: Adjusted coefficient of determination for model context.\nR2_Pred::AbstractFloat: Predictive cross-validation coefficient of determination (Q²).\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Formatted Plotly figure ready for interactive web display.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderFit_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderFit_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderFit_DDEF(Y_Real::AbstractVector{Float64}, Y_Pred::AbstractVector{Float64}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender a scatter plot comparing experimental response observations against model-predicted values. Overlays a diagonal 45-degree dashed reference line to visualize regression accuracy and prediction dispersion.\n\nArguments\n\nY_Real::AbstractVector{Float64}: Observed experimental response measurements.\nY_Pred::AbstractVector{Float64}: Model-estimated response values.\nOutName::AbstractString: Response variable name.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Plotly scatter figure with diagnostic reference styling.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderQQ_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderQQ_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderQQ_DDEF(Residuals::AbstractVector{Float64}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender a normal probability Quantile-Quantile (Q-Q) diagnostic plot for model regression residuals. Compares standardised residuals against theoretical normal distribution quantiles to verify error normality.\n\nArguments\n\nResiduals::AbstractVector{Float64}: Raw regression residuals (Y_observed - Y_predicted).\nOutName::AbstractString: Response variable name.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Diagnostic Q-Q plot with theoretical reference line.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderResiduals_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderResiduals_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderResiduals_DDEF(Y_Pred::AbstractVector{Float64}, Residuals::AbstractVector{Float64}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender a residuals versus predicted response values diagnostic plot to evaluate variance homogeneity. Displays error dispersion around a zero-deviation baseline to diagnose heteroscedasticity and non-linear drift.\n\nArguments\n\nY_Pred::AbstractVector{Float64}: Model-estimated response predictions.\nResiduals::AbstractVector{Float64}: Model residual errors.\nOutName::AbstractString: Response variable name.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Scatter plot formatted for residual variance analysis.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderSensitivity_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderSensitivity_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderSensitivity_DDEF(Sens::AbstractVector{Float64}, InNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender a bar chart displaying fractional percentage contributions of experimental factors from local sensitivity analysis. Normalises partial derivative gradient contributions to rank dominant drivers influencing the response surface.\n\nArguments\n\nSens::AbstractVector{Float64}: Fractional sensitivity values summing to 1.0.\nInNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor labels.\nOutName::AbstractString: Response variable name.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Bar chart displaying relative factor influence percentages.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderDesignSpace_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderDesignSpace_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderDesignSpace_DDEF(Models, Goals, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Idx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Lbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, Leaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame=DataFrame()) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender multi-slice 3D desirability space surfaces representing composite multi-criteria optimisation feasibility. Displays simultaneous horizontal planar slices through the third factor dimension with optional leader formulation markers.\n\nArguments\n\nModels: Vector of active regression model dictionaries.\nGoals: Multi-response optimisation goal specifications.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental factor coordinate matrix.\nIdx::AbstractVector{<:Integer}: Indices of factors chosen for x and y planar projection.\nLbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor axis labels.\nLeaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame: Formulations table containing candidate leader runs (default: empty).\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: 3D multi-surface desirability exploration figure.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderFeasibleSpace_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderFeasibleSpace_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderFeasibleSpace_DDEF(Models, Goals, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, InNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, Leaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame=DataFrame()) -> Tuple{PlotlyJS.Plot, String}\n\nRender a 3D volumetric shell enclosing factor regions that simultaneously satisfy all unweighted response specification limits. Discretises the experimental bounding box into a 3D coordinate lattice to delineate the unconstrained feasible operating domain.\n\nArguments\n\nModels: Vector of active fitted regression model dictionaries.\nGoals: Multi-response optimisation goal specifications.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental factor coordinate matrix.\nInNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor dimension names.\nLeaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame: Formulations table containing candidate leader runs (default: empty).\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{PlotlyJS.Plot, String}: Pair containing the 3D volume figure and the feasible space volume occupancy percentage string.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderOptimalZone_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderOptimalZone_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderOptimalZone_DDEF(Models, Goals, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, InNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, Leaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame=DataFrame()) -> Tuple{PlotlyJS.Plot, String}\n\nRender a 3D volumetric iso-surface enclosing factor regions meeting composite multi-criteria desirability thresholds. Discretises the experimental factor space into a 3D coordinate lattice and identifies contiguous high-desirability zones evaluating weighted multi-response trade-offs.\n\nArguments\n\nModels: Vector of active fitted regression model dictionaries.\nGoals: Multi-response optimisation goal specifications.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental factor coordinate matrix.\nInNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor dimension names.\nLeaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame: Formulations table containing candidate leader runs (default: empty).\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{PlotlyJS.Plot, String}: Pair containing the 3D volume figure and the optimal zone volume occupancy percentage string.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderSweetSpot_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderSweetSpot_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderSweetSpot_DDEF(Models, Goals, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, InNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, Leaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame=DataFrame()) -> Tuple{PlotlyJS.Plot, String}\n\nRender a 3D volumetric sweet spot representing the 95% threshold of peak composite desirability. Isolates the ultra-optimal core factor space where multi-response trade-offs attain elite desirability performance.\n\nArguments\n\nModels: Vector of active fitted regression model dictionaries.\nGoals: Multi-response optimisation goal specifications.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental factor coordinate matrix.\nInNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor dimension names.\nLeaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame: Formulations table containing candidate leader runs (default: empty).\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{PlotlyJS.Plot, String}: Pair containing the 3D volume figure and the sweet spot volume occupancy percentage string.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderCandidates_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderCandidates_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderCandidates_DDEF(Models, Goals, X::AbstractMatrix{Float64}, Idx::Union{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Tuple{Integer, Integer}}, Lbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, Leaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame=DataFrame()) -> Tuple{PlotlyJS.Plot, String}\n\nVisualise top-quartile candidate solution spaces isolating high-desirability regions within the design space. Applies alpha-thresholded Viridis shading to mask sub-optimal zones and highlights leader coordinate points.\n\nArguments\n\nModels: Vector of fitted regression models.\nGoals: Multi-response optimisation goals.\nX::AbstractMatrix{Float64}: Experimental design coordinates.\nIdx::Union{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Tuple{Integer, Integer}}: Planar factor coordinate indices.\nLbls::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor dimension names.\nLeaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame: Candidate leader records table (default: empty).\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{PlotlyJS.Plot, String}: Pair containing the Plotly figure and the percentage volume string.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderInteractionMatrix_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_RenderInteractionMatrix_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_RenderInteractionMatrix_DDEF(Model::AbstractDict, InNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, OutName::AbstractString) -> PlotlyJS.Plot\n\nRender an interaction coefficient heatmap displaying pairwise synergy and antagonism between experimental factors. Extracts off-diagonal second-order model coefficients and normalises them to evaluate relative coupling magnitude.\n\nArguments\n\nModel::AbstractDict: Fitted quadratic regression model dictionary.\nInNames::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Factor component labels.\nOutName::AbstractString: Response variable name.\n\nReturns\n\nPlotlyJS.Plot: Heatmap figure indicating interaction magnitudes and directions.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_Render_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_Render_DDEF","category":"function","text":"ARTS_Render_DDEF(Models, X, Y, InNames, OutNames, Goals, R2s, Q2s, Opts, Leaders_DF::AbstractDataFrame=DataFrame(), Sens::AbstractVector=Vector{Vector{Float64}}[], Residuals::AbstractVector=Vector{Vector{Float64}}[]) -> Vector{Dict{String, Any}}\n\nExecute the parallel visual rendering orchestrator generating publication-grade charts for all modelled responses. Dispatches multi-threaded workers across Pareto charts, surface projections, residual diagnostics, and decision spaces.\n\nArguments\n\nModels: Vector of regression model dictionaries.\nX: Factor coordinate data.\nY: Response measurement data.\nInNames: Factor component labels.\nOutNames: Response variable labels.\nGoals: Multi-response optimisation specifications.\nR2s: Adjusted R² values for each response.\nQ2s: Cross-validated Q² values for each response.\nOpts: Rendering and pipeline options dictionary.\nLeaders_DF: Candidate leader records table (default: empty).\nSens: Factor sensitivity vectors per response (default: empty).\nResiduals: Residual error vectors per response (default: empty).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Dict{String, Any}}: Collection of generated plot descriptors with type tags and Plotly figures.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_PlotPriority_DDEC","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Lib_Arts.ARTS_PlotPriority_DDEC","category":"constant","text":"ARTS_PlotPriority_DDEC\n\nConstant dictionary mapping visual analytical plot types to their deterministic  carousel display and sorting priority order (1 to 15).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_ApplyACTA_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_ApplyACTA_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_ApplyACTA_DDEF(LeaderInfo::Dict, ContractionFactor::Float64=0.5, TranslationFactor::Float64=0.0) -> Vector{Dict{String, Any}}\n\nApply Adaptive Contraction & Translation Algorithm (ACTA) to all variable factors in a configuration set. Iterates over continuous formulation ingredients, updating discrete boundary triplets based on leader coordinates.\n\nArguments\n\nLeaderInfo::Dict: Leader metadata containing \"OldConfig\" and \"Vals\".\nContractionFactor::Float64: Contraction ratio applied to factor level spans (default: 0.5).\nTranslationFactor::Float64: Offset ratio applied to centre coordinates (default: 0.0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Dict{String, Any}}: Updated factor configuration list with contracted/translated levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF(Val::Real, L_Old::Vector{Float64}, Contraction::Real, Translation::Real, MinLimit::Real=0.0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCalculate updated continuous factor levels [L1, L2, L3] via contraction around an optimum and directional translation. Enforces non-negative physical limits and boundary clamping to maintain valid experimental formulation spaces.\n\nArguments\n\nVal::Real: Selected leader coordinate value.\nL_Old::Vector{Float64}: Previous 3-level boundaries [L1, L2, L3].\nContraction::Real: Domain contraction ratio in (0.0, 1.0].\nTranslation::Real: Directional translation factor in [-1.0, 1.0].\nMinLimit::Real: Minimum physical boundary clamp (default: 0.0).\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Float64}: 3-element vector containing updated [lower, centre, upper] levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF(Val::Real, Alpha::Real, Beta::Real) -> Float64\n\nApply the Affine Space Transformation Model (ASTM: y = Alpha * Val + Beta) to project factor levels across experimental domains. Performs linear scaling and translation to adapt formulation boundaries when switching equipment, scales, or reactors.\n\nArguments\n\nVal::Real: Source factor coordinate.\nAlpha::Real: Affine scaling factor.\nBeta::Real: Affine translation offset.\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Transformed coordinate value.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_ValidateASTM_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_ValidateASTM_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_ValidateASTM_DDEF(Val::Real, MinLimit::Real, MaxLimit::Real) -> Tuple{Bool, Float64, String}\n\nValidate and clamp an ASTM-transformed value against physical experimental bounds. Prevents projected factor levels from violating laboratory operating limits or reagent solubility thresholds.\n\nArguments\n\nVal::Real: Transformed coordinate value.\nMinLimit::Real: Minimum admissible physical threshold.\nMaxLimit::Real: Maximum admissible physical threshold.\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Bool, Float64, String}: (is_valid, clamped_value, warning_message).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_AuditLeader_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_AuditLeader_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_AuditLeader_DDEF(LeaderVal::Real, CurrentRange::Vector{Float64}) -> Tuple{Bool, String}\n\nEvaluate whether a chosen leader factor coordinate is in proximity to boundary limits, detecting potential space clipping. Flags coordinates lying dangerously close to the lower or upper physical thresholds to protect subsequent phase search volumes.\n\nArguments\n\nLeaderVal::Real: Candidate optimal factor coordinate.\nCurrentRange::Vector{Float64}: Current factor domain range [min, mid, max].\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{Bool, String}: (is_safe, alert_message).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_BuildIPKT_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_BuildIPKT_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_BuildIPKT_DDEF(MasterFile, CurrentPhase, SelectedLeaderID=\"\", ContractionFactor=0.5, TranslationFactor=0.0) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nConstruct sequential phase transition boundaries by applying ACTA (Adaptive Contraction & Translation Algorithm). Extracts the selected leader run, scales continuous factor spreads, and computes contracted coordinate bounds for the subsequent design.\n\nArguments\n\nMasterFile: Path to master workbook.\nCurrentPhase: Current experimental phase identifier.\nSelectedLeaderID: Optional leader identifier (defaults to highest desirability run).\nContractionFactor: Search domain scaling factor in (0.0, 1.0] (default: 0.5).\nTranslationFactor: Search domain shift factor in [-1.0, 1.0] (default: 0.0).\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Dictionary containing adapted factor configurations, boundary alerts, and leader coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_CommitIPKT_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_CommitIPKT_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_CommitIPKT_DDEF(MasterFile, CurrentPhase, SelectedLeaderID=\"\", ContractionFactor=0.5, Method=\"TL09\", TranslationFactor=0.0; kwargs...) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nFinalise and persist sequential phase transition configurations and generated design matrices into the master workbook. Generates the new experimental protocol sheet, updates configuration tracking records, and logs the evolutionary step.\n\nArguments\n\nMasterFile: Path to master workbook.\nCurrentPhase: Active experimental phase code.\nSelectedLeaderID: Run identifier to advance as phase leader.\nContractionFactor: ACTA search space contraction ratio.\nMethod: Design method code for subsequent phase (\"TL09\", \"BB15\", \"CD17\", \"DF14\").\nTranslationFactor: ACTA search space translation offset.\n\nKeywords\n\nDirection: Directional vector for \"DF14\" design method.\nCustomConfig: Optional overridden factor boundaries and units.\nProjectName: Optional project title for metadata tracking.\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Status dictionary with write confirmation and destination sheet details.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_GetCandidates_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_GetCandidates_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_GetCandidates_DDEF(MasterFile, CurrentPhase) -> Vector{Dict{String, Any}}\n\nRetrieve and sort all candidate optimal runs from the designated phase record by composite desirability. Parses the leader sheet in the workbook and returns a descending priority queue of experimental formulations.\n\nArguments\n\nMasterFile: Path to master workbook.\nCurrentPhase: Phase identifier from which candidates are loaded.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Dict{String, Any}}: Sorted array of candidate runs with associated factors, responses, and desirability scores.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_WriteLeaders_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_WriteLeaders_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_WriteLeaders_DDEF(File, Phase, LeadersDF::DataFrame) -> Bool\n\nWrite prioritised candidate runs for the specified phase into the master Excel workbook. Stores evaluated factor combinations and desirability ranks under dedicated leader tracking worksheets.\n\nArguments\n\nFile: Path to target workbook file.\nPhase: Experimental phase identifier.\nLeadersDF::DataFrame: Table containing candidate coordinates, predictions, and desirability scores.\n\nReturns\n\nBool: true if write operation succeeded, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_RenderIPKT_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_RenderIPKT_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_RenderIPKT_DDEF(OldConfig::AbstractVector, NewConfig::AbstractVector, LeaderVals::AbstractVector; kwargs...) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nGenerate a Plotly visual representation of search domain shifts (contraction and translation) between successive phases. Renders relative horizontal bar intervals showing previous boundaries, adapted search ranges, and leader operating points.\n\nArguments\n\nOldConfig::AbstractVector: Previous phase factor level configurations.\nNewConfig::AbstractVector: Adapted phase factor level configurations.\nLeaderVals::AbstractVector: Chosen leader factor coordinates.\n\nKeywords\n\nMethod::AbstractString: Design method for new phase (default: \"TL09\").\nDirection::AbstractVector: Direction vector for directional designs.\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Any}: Dictionary containing Plotly \"data\" traces and \"layout\" specifications.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_AdvancePhase_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_AdvancePhase_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_AdvancePhase_DDEF(Phase) -> String\n\nDispatch-driven calculation of the subsequent target phase identifier.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_GetPhaseType_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Flow.FLOW_GetPhaseType_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FLOW_GetPhaseType_DDEF(Phase) -> FLOW_AbstractPhase_DDET\n\nResolve string phase representation into a typed phase trait.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_ReadExcel_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_ReadExcel_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FAST_ReadExcel_DDEF(FilePath, SheetName::AbstractString) -> DataFrame\n\nRead an Excel workbook worksheet into a DataFrame with normalised header symbols. Validates structural columns, strips invalid characters, and handles transient file-access retries.\n\nArguments\n\nFilePath: Path to workbook file (returns empty DataFrame if nothing or non-existent).\nSheetName::AbstractString: Target worksheet name.\n\nReturns\n\nDataFrame: Extracted sheet tabular data.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_WriteExcel_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_WriteExcel_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FAST_WriteExcel_DDEF(File::AbstractString, Updates::Dict{<:AbstractString, DataFrame}) -> Nothing\n\nAtomically write or update sheets in an Excel workbook with transient backup and file-lock retry handling. Merges existing sheets, sanitises IEEE 754 numeric tokens, and auto-fits column widths via OpenXML repackaging.\n\nArguments\n\nFile::AbstractString: Target workbook path.\nUpdates::Dict{<:AbstractString, DataFrame}: Mapping from worksheet names to DataFrames to write or update.\n\nReturns\n\nNothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_InitialiseMaster_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_InitialiseMaster_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FAST_InitialiseMaster_DDEF(File::String, InNames::Vector{String}, OutNames::Vector{String}, DesignData::DataFrame, Config::Dict{String,Any}=Dict{String,Any}()) -> Bool\n\nConstruct and initialise the master Excel workbook containing design runs, config metadata, and audit records. Merges incoming experimental topologies with existing historical phases and preserves JSON-encoded configuration state.\n\nArguments\n\nFile::String: Destination path for master workbook.\nInNames::Vector{String}: Experimental factor names.\nOutNames::Vector{String}: Response metric names.\nDesignData::DataFrame: Formatted experimental design run table.\nConfig::Dict{String, Any}: Formulation boundaries and operational settings (default: empty dictionary).\n\nReturns\n\nBool: true if initialisation succeeded, false otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_GenSmartName_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_GenSmartName_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FAST_GenSmartName_DDEF(Project::AbstractString, Phase::AbstractString, Tag::AbstractString, Ext::AbstractString=\"xlsx\") -> String\n\nGenerate a standardised, timestamped filename for protocol workbooks and reports. Sanitises non-ASCII characters, encodes phase and subproject tags, and appends chronological timestamps.\n\nArguments\n\nProject::AbstractString: Project name identifier.\nPhase::AbstractString: Phase code (e.g. \"Phase1\").\nTag::AbstractString: Context tag (e.g. \"Deck\", \"Lens\").\nExt::AbstractString: File extension without leading dot (default: \"xlsx\").\n\nReturns\n\nString: Formatted filename matching DoECISORY_<Project>_<Phase>_<Tag>_<Timestamp>.<Ext>.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_SafeNum_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_SafeNum_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FAST_SafeNum_DDEF(x) -> Float64\n\nConvert numeric, string, or missing inputs into a valid Float64 representation, returning NaN on failure. Normalises European comma decimals, hyphen placeholders, and sentinel tokens to ensure scientific arithmetic safety.\n\nArguments\n\nx: Input value to parse (supports commas, decimals, hyphens, and missing tokens).\n\nReturns\n\nFloat64: Parsed numerical value or NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_Log_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_Log_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FAST_Log_DDEF(Source, Event, [Detail], [Type])\n\nStandardised console logging with ANSI colour support and timestamps. Bridged to Value-Dispatch.\n\n\n\n\n\nFAST_Log_DDEF(Source::AbstractString, Event::AbstractString, Detail::Any=\"\", Type::AbstractString=\"INFO\") -> Nothing\n\nFormat and emit a structured console telemetry log with timestamp and severity colour coding. Dispatches value types to render ANSI terminal escapes for real-time operation tracking.\n\nArguments\n\nSource::AbstractString: Emitting subsystem tag (e.g. \"CORE\", \"VISE\", \"FAST\").\nEvent::AbstractString: Operation or event identifier.\nDetail::Any: Contextual message, metric, or status payload (default: \"\").\nType::AbstractString: Severity level (\"INFO\", \"OK\", \"WAIT\", \"WARN\", or \"FAIL\").\n\nReturns\n\nNothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_Data_DDEC","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_Data_DDEC","category":"constant","text":"FAST_Data_DDEC\n\nGlobal constant instance of FAST_Catalogue_DDES providing system-wide sheet names, column headers, status indicators, and design topology identifiers.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF","page":"API Reference","location":"api/#DoECISORY.Sys_Fast.FAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF","category":"function","text":"FAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF(Images::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, LayoutType::Symbol=:Grid2x3) -> String\n\nAssemble a composite SVG figure plate according to the 145 mm publication standard. Accepts either a vector of base64 PNG data strings or local PNG file paths. Supported LayoutTypes:\n\n:Grid2x3 (default): 6 panels (2 rows × 3 columns, 145 mm × 115.200 mm), labelled A to F.\n:Grid3x3: 9 panels (3 rows × 3 columns, 145 mm × 172.800 mm), labelled A to I.\n:Strip1x3: 3 panels (1 row × 3 columns, 145 mm × 57.600 mm), labelled A to C.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Graphical Interface Guide","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#Graphical-Interface-Guide","category":"section","text":"This guide provides an operational manual for using DoECISORY.jl via its interactive browser interface. \n\nAll steps are managed through input forms, visual controls, and Microsoft Excel workbooks without requiring command-line interaction.\n\n"},{"title":"1. Application Startup","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#1.-Application-Startup","category":"section","text":"From a Julia environment or terminal:\n\nusing DoECISORY\nlaunch()\n\nYour default web browser will open the application at http://127.0.0.1:8060. If port 8060 is occupied on a shared workstation or server, CALL_ResolvePort_DDEF automatically scans and binds to an open TCP port, reporting the active address in the terminal.\n\n"},{"title":"2. Experimental Design (Deck)","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#2.-Experimental-Design-(Deck)","category":"section","text":"The Deck workspace manages formulation setup, mass balance auditing, and experimental matrix generation:\n\n  [1. Define Factors] ───► Enter Factor Names, Physical Units, and [Min, Mid, Max] Levels\n            │\n  [2. Select Design]  ───► Choose Design Geometry (BB15, CD17, TL09, or DF14 Directional)\n            │\n  [3. Recipe Audit]   ───► Specify Precursor, Buffer, Excipients & Run Stoichiometric Check\n            │\n  [4. Export Excel]   ───► Click \"Generate & Download Protocol Workbook (.xlsx)\""},{"title":"2.1. Defining Ingredients & Stoichiometry","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#2.1.-Defining-Ingredients-and-Stoichiometry","category":"section","text":"In the Formulation Configuration card, input each reagent:\nActive Precursor (VAR): Continuous variables subject to experimental variation.\nFixed Salts/Buffers (FIX): Constant formulation components.\nDiluent/Filler (FILL): Solvent or carrier adjusted to maintain a constant total volume.\nIf working with short-lived radionuclides (e.g. ^18textF, ^68textGa), toggle Is Radioactive and enter the physical half-life.\nClick Audit Mass Balance to verify that concentration and total volume constraints are strictly satisfied."},{"title":"2.2. Generating the Protocol Sheet","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#2.2.-Generating-the-Protocol-Sheet","category":"section","text":"Select your target design geometry (e.g. BB15 Box-Behnken).\nClick Download Protocol Workbook. An Excel file (DoECISORY_Protocol.xlsx) is downloaded containing pre-formatted, colour-coded worksheets ready for benchtop execution.\n\n"},{"title":"3. Data Analysis and Modelling (Lens)","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#3.-Data-Analysis-and-Modelling-(Lens)","category":"section","text":"Once laboratory runs are performed and analytical results obtained:\n\n  [1. Upload Results] ───► Drag and drop the completed Excel workbook into the Lens Dropzone\n            │\n  [2. Set Objectives] ───► Define Min/Max targets, enable DCYP time-decay penalty if needed  \n            │\n  [3. View Report]    ───► Open the Statistical Summary Modal, review ANOVA and Q²\n            │\n  [4. 3D Inspection]  ───► Rotate 3D response surfaces, inspect equipotential contour slices\n            │\n  [5. Candidate Pool] ───► Select optimal leader (TOP-01, INP-Precursor, or OUT-Impurity)"},{"title":"3.1. Uploading Experimental Data","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#3.1.-Uploading-Experimental-Data","category":"section","text":"Navigate to the Lens tab.\nDrag and drop your completed workbook. The system parses all input columns, measured outputs, and any recorded preparation/measurement latency times (TIME_FORW_MINS_, TIME_REVE_MINS_).\nPrecursor forward decay and analytical EOS reverse decay corrections are applied automatically via VISE_ApplyFRDecay_DDEF if configured."},{"title":"3.2. Interactive Visualisation & Graph Navigation","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#3.2.-Interactive-Visualisation-and-Graph-Navigation","category":"section","text":"Graph Index Quick Navigation: Click any category badge above the canvas (Pareto, Factor Slice, Factor Contour, Interaction Matrix, Diagnostic Fits, Feasible Space, Sweet Spot) to jump directly to the first plot of that analytical group in the carousel.\n3D Surface Rotation: Click and drag on the 3D surface plot to inspect topography, response peaks, and saddle points.\nFactor Slicing: Use the slider below the graph to fix the third factor coordinate and evaluate 2D cross-sectional contours."},{"title":"3.3. Statistical Diagnostics and Portfolio Export","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#3.3.-Statistical-Diagnostics-and-Portfolio-Export","category":"section","text":"Click Analytical Summary to open the full statistical report modal.\nThe modal displays:\nCross-validated Q^2 and R^2 values,\nANOVA F-tests and Lack-of-Fit indicators,\nVariance Inflation Factors (VIF) to ensure absence of collinear distortion.\nExporting Analytics:\nDownload Report (.txt): Saves the complete 7-stage interpreted scientific report.\nDownload Plot (.png): Exports the currently active canvas as a high-resolution raster image (960 times 1080textpx).\nDownload Portfolio (.zip): Generates a complete publication archive containing all 12 composite 145 mm SVG plates (PLATE11 to PLATE60), the analytical report (REPORT.txt), and structured Excel data (ANALYTICS.xlsx).\n\n"},{"title":"4. Sequential Phase Transition (ACTA & ASTM)","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#4.-Sequential-Phase-Transition-(ACTA-and-ASTM)","category":"section","text":"To advance from Phase 1 to Phase 2:\n\nIn the Candidate Leaders Table, review the ranked solutions:\nTOP-01*: Global unconstrained composite desirability.\nINP-Precursor: Lowest precursor consumption within 90 of maximum desirability.\nINP-Temperature: Lowest thermal load candidate.\nOUT-Impurity: Lowest radiolytic impurity candidate.\nSelect your preferred candidate row.\nACTA Contraction Factor (c): Adjust the slider to set the search range contraction:\nc = 050\n(Default): Contracts the factor range by 50% around the selected leader.\nc = 075\n: Mild 25% refinement for broader confirmation.\nc = 025\n: Aggressive 75% zoom for precise fine-tuning.\nReview the IPKT Interval Bar Chart to visually confirm that the new boundaries are valid and safely clamped away from equipment limits.\nClick Commit Phase Transition & Download Phase 2 Protocol. The updated workbook is immediately generated with Phase 1 variance preserved in metadata.\n\n"},{"title":"5. UI to Engine Parameter Mapping","page":"Graphical Guide","location":"interface/#5.-UI-to-Engine-Parameter-Mapping","category":"section","text":"For researchers coordinating computational scripts and laboratory interfaces, the table below maps each graphical control in Deck and Lens to its underlying functional Julia routine:\n\nGraphical Control / Widget Workspace User Operation Underlying Julia API Function Engine Arguments & Structs\nAudit Mass Balance Button Deck Verifies formulation conservation MOLE_AuditQuick_DDEF TableData::AbstractVector, Vol::Float64, Conc::Float64\nDesign Geometry Dropdown Deck Generates design matrix CORE_GenDesign_DDEF DesignType::String, nFactors::Int, Direction\nFactor Level Inputs [Min, Mid, Max] Deck Maps coded space to units CORE_MapLevels_DDEF X_coded::Matrix, FactorConfig::Vector{Dict}\nDownload Protocol Workbook Button Deck Builds formatted .xlsx FAST_WriteExcel_DDEF File::AbstractString, Updates::Dict\nDataset Ingestion Dropzone Lens Parses sheet & applies decay FAST_ReadExcel_DDEF & VISE_ApplyFRDecay_DDEF df::DataFrame, decay_map::Dict\nModel Type Selector Lens Fits response surfaces VISE_Regress_DDEF X::Matrix, Y::Vector, ModelType::String\nAnalytical Summary Modal Lens Displays 7-stage diagnostic report VISE_GenSciReport_DDEF Res::AbstractDict\nANOVA & Lack-of-Fit Display Lens Evaluates model adequacy VISE_GenANOVATable_DDEF Model::AbstractDict, X_Raw::Any, Y_Raw::Any\nDCYP Kinetic Penalty Toggle Lens Enforces duration trade-off CORE_ApplyDCYP_DDEF Score::Float64, CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES, x\nExecute Optimization Button Lens Solves multi-objective landscape CORE_OptimiseDesirability_DDEF models, goals::Vector{Dict}, bounds\nCandidate Portfolio Selector Lens Extracts multi-criteria leaders FLOW_GetCandidates_DDEF MasterFile, CurrentPhase\nACTA Contraction Slider (c) Lens Adapts domain boundaries FLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF Val, L_Old, Contraction, Translation, MinLimit\nASTM Transformation Inputs Lens Scales and clamps physical space FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF Val::Real, Alpha::Real, Beta::Real\nTransfer Knowledge Button Lens rightarrow Deck Bridges sequential phase FLOW_CommitIPKT_DDEF & FLOW_BuildIPKT_DDEF MasterFile, CurrentPhase, SelectedLeaderID, ContractionFactor\nDownload Portfolio Button Lens Builds 145 mm multi-plate ZIP FAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF Images::Vector{String}, LayoutType::Symbol\nGraph Index Badges Lens Jumps carousel to plot group Client-side DOM Trigger data-page, role=\"button\""},{"title":"Novel Methodological Features","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#Novel-Methodological-Features","category":"section","text":"While classical experimental designs, Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression, and ANOVA procedures follow established literature (detailed in Statistics and API Reference), this section details the four novel methodological features introduced in DoECISORY.jl:\n\nTemporal Latency Adjustments: Accounting for precursor preparation decay and post-reaction analytical measurement delays via half-life (t_12) kinetics.\nDecay-Coupled Yield Penalty (DCYP) & Regularisation: Reconciling chemical conversion duration with continuous half-life loss.\nCandidate Pool Architecture: Deriving a structured portfolio of operational compromises (unconstrained leaders, input conservation, and output specialisation) rather than forcing a single deterministic optimum.\nInterphase Knowledge Transfer (IPKT = ACTA + ASTM): Sequentially transferring historical variance, contracting operational bounds, and transforming coordinates between successive experimental phases.\n\n"},{"title":"1. Temporal Latency Adjustments: Pre- and Post-Reaction Delays","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#1.-Temporal-Latency-Adjustments:-Pre-and-Post-Reaction-Delays","category":"section","text":"In experiments involving radioisotopes or labile chemical compounds with a known half-life (t_12), elapsed time is an active physical variable that alters reagent mass and product yield. Precursor decay prior to reaction initiation and analytical latency following synthesis completion are managed via VISE_ApplyFRDecay_DDEF and MOLE_CalcRadioDecay_DDEF:\n\nPrecursor Preparation Delay (Delta t_textforw): The elapsed time between stock precursor calibration and addition to the reaction vessel at t_0.\nReaction Duration (Delta t_textreaction): The controlled residence time in the reactor.\nMeasurement Latency (Delta t_textreve): The post-reaction elapsed time between synthesis completion (or reaction quenching) and analytical quantification (e.g. HPLC, SPE, or dose calibrator)."},{"title":"Exponential Half-Life Principle","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#Exponential-Half-Life-Principle","category":"section","text":"Any decaying or labile species experiences depletion governed by its characteristic half-life (t_12):\n\nC(t) = C_0 cdot exp(-lambda cdot t) = C_0 cdot 2^-t  t_12 quad textwhere  lambda = fracln 2t_12\n\nThe half-life parameter t_12 operates across two primary scientific paradigms:\n\nScientific Paradigm Sensitive / Labile Entity Physical Meaning of t_12 Practical Laboratory Consequence\nRadiopharmaceutical Chemistry & Nuclear Medicine Short-lived radionuclide (^18textF, ^68textGa, ^11textC, ^177textLu, etc.) Physical isotope half-life (invariant nuclear constant) Precursor radioactive decay pre-synthesis & analytical loss during HPLC waiting queue\nLabile Chemical Synthesis & Sensitive APIs Unstable reactive intermediate, photolabile or hydrolytically labile active molecule Measured chemical degradation half-life under operating conditions Active substrate loss prior to reactor charging & degradation during autosampler holding delay"},{"title":"1.1. Forward Precursor Degradation Correction","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#1.1.-Forward-Precursor-Degradation-Correction","category":"section","text":"Between reagent calibration and reaction initiation (t_0), elapsed preparation time (Delta t_textforw) leads to substrate loss.\n\nIf uncorrected, the nominal concentration or activity recorded in the design matrix introduces systematic bias into the regression model. The true starting quantity C_textactual is computed using MOLE_CalcRadioDecay_DDEF with Reverse=false:\n\nC_textactual = C_textnominal cdot exp(-lambda cdot Delta t_textforw)\n\nwhere:\n\nC_textnominal\nis the nominal precursor activity, mass, or concentration,\nlambda = fracln 2t_12\nis the physical or experimental decay constant derived from the half-life (t_12),\nDelta t_textforw\nis the preparation delay.\n\nThe training matrix column (ACTUAL_<Precursor>) is populated with these values, removing preparation latency variance from the model.\n\n[!NOTE] Primary Application: Radiopharmaceutical Benchmark In PET/SPECT radiotracer synthesis, elapsed time between radionuclide calibration and vessel charging (Delta t_textforw) leads to physical decay of radioactive atoms. For ^18textF (t_12 = 10977textmin), a 15-minute bench delay causes an approximate 91 loss of starting precursor activity. Correcting with Reverse=false reconstructs true starting radioactivity A_textactual, removing benchtop timing noise from the design matrix.\n\n[!TIP] General Chemical Analogy: Labile Reagents & Reactive Intermediates In organic synthesis and pharmaceutical formulation, reactive intermediates (e.g. moisture-sensitive acid chlorides, diazo reagents, or short-lived free radicals) or labile active pharmaceutical ingredients (APIs) often have documented chemical degradation half-lives (t_12). By supplying this measured half-life to the engine, nominal amounts are adjusted to actual effective concentrations at the moment of addition."},{"title":"1.2. Reverse Analyte Restoration (Reference State Alignment)","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#1.2.-Reverse-Analyte-Restoration-(Reference-State-Alignment)","category":"section","text":"Following synthesis completion or reaction quenching, analytical chromatography and quantification involve variable delay (Delta t_textreve).\n\nTo compare product yields across all design runs on an equivalent baseline, raw measured quantity C_textmeasured is reconstructed back to the reference state (End of Synthesis - EOS, or quenching moment) using MOLE_CalcRadioDecay_DDEF with Reverse=true:\n\nC_textref = C_textmeasured cdot exp(+lambda cdot Delta t_textreve)\n\nUpdating the response column (ACTUAL_<Output>) isolates chemical conversion efficiency from post-synthesis analytical waiting times.\n\n[!NOTE] Radiopharmaceutical Benchmark Analytical HPLC, solid-phase extraction (SPE) purification, and dose calibration involve variable holding delays (Delta t_textreve) between sequential runs. Reconstructing measured activity back to EOS via Reverse=true decouples chemical radiolabelling yield from post-synthesis analytical waiting queues.\n\n[!TIP] General Analytical Chemistry Analogy When quantifying hydrolytically or photolytically unstable products, samples standing in an automated autosampler queue undergo progressive degradation. Reverse correction restores the analyte concentration back to the exact quenching time (t_textquench).\n\n"},{"title":"2. Kinetic Trade-Off: Decay-Coupled Yield Penalty (DCYP)","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#2.-Kinetic-Trade-Off:-Decay-Coupled-Yield-Penalty-(DCYP)","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"2.1. Decay-Coupled Yield Penalty (DCYP)","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#2.1.-Decay-Coupled-Yield-Penalty-(DCYP)","category":"section","text":"During reaction execution, chemical transformation typically exhibits an asymptotic plateau or sigmoidal rise with respect to duration (Delta t_textreaction). However, exponential half-life decay concurrently diminishes the remaining active payload:\n\nY_textnet(t) = Y_textchemical(t) cdot exp(-lambda cdot t) = Y_textchemical(t) cdot 2^-t  t_12\n\nOptimising solely for percentage conversion yield (Y_textchemical) leads to artificially prolonged reaction times that produce severely degraded net active product or depleted radiotracer doses.\n\nIn DoECISORY.jl, the Decay-Coupled Yield Penalty (DCYP) integrates physical kinetic decay directly into the multi-criteria composite desirability function D:\n\nD_textadjusted = D cdot exp(-lambda cdot Delta t_textreaction)\n\nBy embedding this penalty into both gradient-free metaheuristics and continuous landscape evaluations, DoECISORY.jl identifies the precise kinetic optimum that balances reaction completion against exponential first-order loss.\n\n[!NOTE] Radiopharmaceutical Application For short-lived radionuclides (^11textC, ^18textF, ^68textGa), a reaction taking 45 minutes to reach 90% radiochemical conversion will yield significantly less net injectable radioactivity than a 15-minute reaction reaching 75% conversion, due to continuous half-life decay. DCYP mathematically arbitrates this dilemma to maximise injectable patient dose at EOS.\n\n[!TIP] Labile Intermediate & Sensitive API Analogy When synthesising products from unstable or thermally delicate active ingredients with a known degradation half-life t_12, extending reaction duration past the kinetic peak degrades the isolated product. DCYP balances chemical conversion rate against compound stability to identify the true productive optimum."},{"title":"2.2. Gaussian Neighbour-Weighting Regularisation","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#2.2.-Gaussian-Neighbour-Weighting-Regularisation","category":"section","text":"Standard Derringer-Suich desirability functions combine individual desirability transformations d_i(y_i) via geometric averaging:\n\nD = left( prod_i=1^k d_i(y_i)^w_i right)^frac1sum w_i\n\nSharp boundaries or disparate objective weightings can induce numerical singularities and noisy objective landscapes that trap gradient or evolutionary search engines in suboptimal local modes.\n\nTo guarantee numerical stability, DoECISORY.jl applies a Gaussian Neighbour-Weighting Regularisation scheme across a discrete scale of importance weights:\n\nmathbfw_textstar = 050 075 100 150 200\n\nFor each user-specified weight w_i, the neighbouring lower (w_m), current (w_c), and upper (w_p) weights are extracted. The regularised composite desirability is calculated by blending standard exponential weighting with local neighbour-smoothed averaging:\n\nu = d_i(y_i)^fracw_ck quad e = fracd_i(y_i)^fracw_mk + d_i(y_i)^fracw_ck + d_i(y_i)^fracw_pk3\n\ntextScore_textcomposite = 050 cdot prod_i=1^k u_i + 050 cdot prod_i=1^k e_i\n\nThis regularisation smoothens narrow ridges in the multi-response landscape, enabling both deterministic grid searches and metaheuristic solvers to converge reliably upon stable global solutions.\n\n"},{"title":"3. Candidate Pool Generation and Selection","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#3.-Candidate-Pool-Generation-and-Selection","category":"section","text":"Rather than restricting the output to a single theoretical point x^*, DoECISORY.jl constructs a structured Candidate Pool covering distinct operational compromises: global optimality, input conservation, and output specialisation."},{"title":"3.1. Search Strategy and Deduplication","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#3.1.-Search-Strategy-and-Deduplication","category":"section","text":"Continuous Metaheuristic Optimisation: Explores the continuous parameter space via evolutionary metaheuristics (BlackBoxOptim.jl), locating the global mathematical peak coordinate mathbfx_textBBO and score S_textBBO.\nDeterministic High-Density Grid: Evaluates the 3-factor space across a dense grid (21^3 = 9261 or 41^3 = 68921 nodes), computing deterministic multi-model predictions and desirability scores.\nEuclidean Deduplication: Grid points are checked against the BBO peak using normalised Euclidean distance:\ntextdist(mathbfx_i mathbfx_textBBO) = sum_j=1^d fracx_ij - x_textBBOjU_j - L_j  001\nPoints clustering within 1 of the BBO optimum are pruned to avoid redundant solutions."},{"title":"3.2. Candidate Classification","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#3.2.-Candidate-Classification","category":"section","text":"From the deduplicated search results, DoECISORY.jl builds a multi-criteria portfolio of candidates rather than forcing a single deterministic compromise:\n\n1. Absolute Global Leaders (TOP-01 to TOP-08): The continuous metaheuristic solution (mathbfx_textBBO) and top discrete grid nodes are pooled together and sorted descending by composite desirability. Up to 8 unconstrained candidates (TOP-01 through TOP-08) are extracted:\nThe Asterisk (*) Identifier: The candidate originating from continuous BlackBoxOptim carries an asterisk (e.g. TOP-01* or TOP-02*). While BBO often captures the global summit (TOP-01*), under tight computational time budgets or rugged multimodal surfaces, a high-density grid node may outscore it, placing BBO at TOP-02* or lower.\nDiscrete Grid Leaders: The remaining unflagged entries represent distinct discrete grid coordinates that maintain spatial diversity (textdist  001) across the peak desirability region.\n2. Input Minimisation Leaders (INP-<Factor>): Isolated within the top performance tier (D(mathbfx) ge 090 cdot D_max). For each active experimental factor j in 1 dots d, the algorithm extracts the coordinate that individually minimises that specific input:\nmathbfx_textINP-j = argmin_mathbfx in textTier_90 x_j\nINP-X1 (e.g. INP-Temperature): Formulations operating at minimal thermal load to prevent substrate decomposition, thermal side-reactions, or excessive energy consumption.\nINP-X2 (e.g. INP-Time): Formulations with minimal residence time, reducing physical radioactive decay, chemical degradation during holding, and overall cycle duration.\nINP-X3 (e.g. INP-Precursor): Green chemistry formulations conserving scarce, costly, or high-purity active pharmaceutical ingredients (APIs).\n3. Output Specialisation Leaders (OUT-<Response>): Identified within the same 90 desirability tier by evaluating which candidate maximises or minimises individual quality attributes:\nOUT-Y1 (e.g. OUT-Conversion): Maximises overall chemical conversion or crude radiochemical incorporation.\nOUT-Y2 (e.g. OUT-Yield): Maximises net isolated product recovery or end-of-synthesis (EOS) yield.\nOUT-Y3 (e.g. OUT-Impurity): Minimises radiolytic degradation by-products, fragments, or chemical impurities.\n\nThis multi-faceted portfolio allows the research team to select a leader tailored to specific laboratory limitations (e.g. reagent scarcity, heating constraints, or impurity limits) before advancing to the next sequential phase.\n\n"},{"title":"4. Sequential Interphase Transfer (ACTA and ASTM)","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#4.-Sequential-Interphase-Transfer-(ACTA-and-ASTM)","category":"section","text":"The Interphase Knowledge Transfer (IPKT) framework connects sequential experimental phases through a two-step mathematical operator: ACTA handles intra-space domain adaptation, while ASTM executes cross-system affine mapping and space regeneration."},{"title":"4.1. Domain Adaptation (ACTA)","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#4.1.-Domain-Adaptation-(ACTA)","category":"section","text":"Given the chosen leader coordinate x_i^* within current bounds L_i U_i, the normalised relative position p_i is evaluated:\n\np_i = fracx_i^* - L_iU_i - L_i\n\nContraction (005 le p_i le 095): The optimum is situated within the internal search volume. The search window is contracted symmetrically around the leader point:\nDelta_textnew = c cdot (U_i - L_i)\nL_i^textnew = x_i^* - fracDelta_textnew2 quad U_i^textnew = x_i^* + fracDelta_textnew2\nThe contraction factor c in (00 10 (default: c = 050, representing a 50 range narrowing) is researcher-controlled via UI slider or script argument.\nTranslation (p_i  005 or p_i  095): The optimum lies on or near the boundary, indicating that the true physical summit may reside outside the current domain. The range width is preserved while the window translates directionally toward the leader:\ndelta = x_i^* - fracL_i + U_i2\nL_i^textnew = L_i + delta quad U_i^textnew = U_i + delta\nAbsolute Clamping: All newly derived boundaries are checked against physical, thermodynamic, and safety limits L_i^textabs U_i^textabs via FLOW_ValidateASTM_DDEF:\nL_i^textclamped = maxleft(L_i^textabs L_i^textnewright) quad U_i^textclamped = minleft(U_i^textabs U_i^textnewright)"},{"title":"4.2. Affine Transformation and Clamping (ASTM)","page":"Methodology","location":"methodology/#4.2.-Affine-Transformation-and-Clamping-(ASTM)","category":"section","text":"Following ACTA boundary adaptation, coordinates undergo affine mapping to accommodate inter-phase scale changes, equipment migrations, or formulation changes:\n\nx_texttransformed = alpha cdot x + beta\n\nThis operator supports four distinct experimental scenarios:\n\nThermodynamic Invariance (alpha = 10 beta = 00): Factors such as temperature or pH that maintain identical physical boundaries across experimental scales are transferred directly.\nKinetic & Volumetric Scaling (alpha neq 10 beta = 00): When transitioning from a 1 mL micro-reactor to a 10 mL preparative vessel, flow rates or reaction times are multiplied by scaling ratio alpha.\nSystematic Offset (alpha = 10 beta neq 00): Incorporates systematic calibration offsets when changing analytical instruments or reagent batches.\nSpace Regeneration & Factor Replacement: When a solvent or catalyst is substituted between phases, common continuous factors retain their historical knowledge, peak positions, and variance through IPKT, while the novel factor is initialised with new physical levels. This avoids resetting the entire experimental campaign."},{"title":"Programmatic Scripting Guide","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#Programmatic-Scripting-Guide","category":"section","text":"This guide covers batch execution, scripted workflows, multi-threading, and system image compilation for DoECISORY.jl.\n\n"},{"title":"1. Programmatic Workflow","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#1.-Programmatic-Workflow","category":"section","text":"DoECISORY.jl provides both high-level and low-level APIs for programmatic execution. Currently, design serves as the primary high-level endpoint for experimental planning, with further high-level APIs planned for future releases. For direct low-level control, routines follow the symbol template:\n\n[DOMAIN]_[PascalCase-Action/Entity]_DDE[F|S|T|C]\n\nHere: [DOMAIN] identifies the subsystem, [PascalCase-Action/Entity] describes the operational action or data entity, DDE marks a DoECISORY-defined construct, and the terminal letter denotes functions (_DDEF), structs (_DDES), abstract types (_DDET), or constants (_DDEC) (e.g. VISE_AnalyseSensitivity_DDEF and CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES)."},{"title":"1.1. Experimental Planning Example with design()","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#1.1.-Experimental-Planning-Example-with-design()","category":"section","text":"The high-level design function constructs experimental matrices, maps physical levels, and evaluates information optimality in a single call:\n\nusing DoECISORY\n\n# 1. Synthesise experimental design matrix & inspect information optimality\nbegin\n  plan = design(:bb15;\n    factors = [\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"],\n    levels  = [(60.0, 90.0, 120.0), (5.0, 15.0, 25.0), (15.0, 25.0, 35.0)]\n  )\n  show(stdout, MIME(\"text/plain\"), plan.dataframe; allrows=true)\n  println()\n  println(\"D-Efficiency                    : \", round(plan.metrics[\"D\"] * 100.0, digits=2), \"%\")\n  println(\"G-Efficiency                    : \", round(plan.metrics[\"G\"] * 100.0, digits=2), \"%\")\n  println(\"Condition No                    : \", round(plan.metrics[\"Condition\"], digits=2))\nend"},{"title":"1.2. Low-Level Modular Algorithmic Workflow","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#1.2.-Low-Level-Modular-Algorithmic-Workflow","category":"section","text":"When custom coordinate manipulation or step-by-step mathematical intervention is required, routines from Lib_Core, Lib_Vise, and Sys_Flow can be invoked directly:\n\nusing DoECISORY\n\nbegin\n# 1. Synthesise experimental design matrix & inspect optimality\n  X_coded = CORE_GenDesign_DDEF(\"BB15\", 3)\n  factor_config = [\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Temperature\", \"Levels\" => [60.0, 90.0, 120.0]),\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Time\",        \"Levels\" => [5.0,  15.0, 25.0]),\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Precursor\",   \"Levels\" => [15.0, 25.0, 35.0])\n  ]\n  X = Matrix{Float64}(CORE_MapLevels_DDEF(X_coded, factor_config))\n  metrics = CORE_CalcDesignMetrics_DDEF(Float64.(X_coded), \"quadratic\")\n  println(\"D-Efficiency                    : \", round(metrics[\"D\"] * 100.0, digits=2), \"%\")\n  println(\"G-Efficiency                    : \", round(metrics[\"G\"] * 100.0, digits=2), \"%\")\n  println(\"Condition No                    : \", round(metrics[\"Condition\"], digits=2))\n\n# 2. Analytical response observations (Y_conv, Y_yield, Y_impur)\n  Y_conv   = [87.4, 53.1, 73.6, 77.2, 94.5, 87.0, 65.3, 82.7, 75.5, 92.1, 87.7, 59.9, 80.4, 70.2, 90.6]\n  Y_yield  = [77.6, 45.2, 66.1, 69.9, 72.4, 77.1, 57.4, 73.8, 67.5, 74.2, 77.9, 51.1, 70.3, 63.4, 73.8]\n  Y_impur  = [2.5,  8.2,  4.8,  6.4,  5.2,  2.2,  6.9,  4.1,  5.6,  3.3,  2.7,  7.6,  5.4,  6.2,  3.9]\n\n# 3. Fit quadratic response surface models (VISE_Regress_DDEF)\n  in_names = [\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"]\n  models = [\n    VISE_Regress_DDEF(X, Y_conv,  \"quadratic\"; InNames=in_names),\n    VISE_Regress_DDEF(X, Y_yield, \"quadratic\"; InNames=in_names),\n    VISE_Regress_DDEF(X, Y_impur, \"quadratic\"; InNames=in_names)\n  ]\n  println(\"Model R² (Conv, Yield, Impurity)  : \", [round(m[\"R2\"]; digits=3) for m in models])\n  println(\"Model RMSE (Conv, Yield, Impurity): \", [round(m[\"RMSE\"]; digits=2) for m in models])\n\n# 4. Multi-response desirability optimisation with 18F DCYP & sensitivity\n  goals = [\n    Dict(\"Type\" => \"Maximise\", \"Min\" => 50.0, \"Max\" => 98.0, \"Target\" => 92.0, \"Weight\" => 1.0),\n    Dict(\"Type\" => \"Maximise\", \"Min\" => 40.0, \"Max\" => 85.0, \"Target\" => 80.0, \"Weight\" => 1.2),\n    Dict(\"Type\" => \"Minimise\", \"Min\" => 1.0,  \"Max\" => 10.0, \"Target\" => 2.0,  \"Weight\" => 1.5)\n  ]\n  dcyp = [CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES(2, log(2.0) / 109.8, \"18F\")]\n  box  = hcat(minimum(X; dims=1)', maximum(X; dims=1)')\n  (opt_x, opt_score) = CORE_OptimiseDesirability_DDEF(models, goals, box; MaxTime=0.4, ModifiersDCYP=dcyp)\n  opt_preds          = [round(VISE_Predict_DDEF(m, reshape(opt_x, 1, 3))[1]; digits=1) for m in models]\n  sensitivities      = VISE_AnalyseSensitivity_DDEF(models[1], opt_x, X)\n  println(\"Multi-Desirability Score        : \", round(opt_score; digits=4))\n  println(\"Optimum (Temp, Time, Precursor) : \", round.(opt_x; digits=2))\n  println(\"Predicted Responses at Optimum  : \", opt_preds)\n  println(\"Factor Sensitivities (%)        : \", round.(sensitivities .* 100.0; digits=1))\n\n# 5. Interphase Knowledge Transfer (IPKT = ACTA + ASTM)\n# Step A: ACTA domain contraction (c = 0.50 around leader)\n  acta_bounds = FLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF(opt_x[1], [60.0, 90.0, 120.0], 0.5, 0.0, 0.0)\n  println(\"ACTA Phase 2 Contraction (Temp) : \", round.(acta_bounds; digits=2))\n\n# Step B: ASTM affine transformation & verification (α = 0.9, β = 9.0)\n  phase2_levels = [FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF(x, 0.9, 9.0) for x in acta_bounds]\n  println(\"ASTM Phase 2 Scaling (Temp)     : \", round.(phase2_levels; digits=2))\nend"},{"title":"1.3. Hybrid Laboratory Workflow (DataFrame Ingestion & Candidate Pool Search)","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#1.3.-Hybrid-Laboratory-Workflow-(DataFrame-Ingestion-and-Candidate-Pool-Search)","category":"section","text":"In modern laboratories, researchers often combine the speed of tabular dataframes with the computational rigor of scripted multi-objective grid optimisation. The snippet below illustrates this end-to-end loop:\n\nusing DoECISORY, DataFrames\n\n# 1. Synthesise or ingest completed laboratory dataset\nplan = design(:bb15;\n  factors = [\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"],\n  levels  = [(60.0, 90.0, 120.0), (5.0, 15.0, 25.0), (15.0, 25.0, 35.0)]\n)\ndf_lab = copy(plan.dataframe)\ndf_lab[!, \"RESULT_Conversion\"] = [87.4, 53.1, 73.6, 77.2, 94.5, 87.0, 65.3, 82.7, 75.5, 92.1, 87.7, 59.9, 80.4, 70.2, 90.6]\ndf_lab[!, \"RESULT_Yield\"]      = [77.6, 45.2, 66.1, 69.9, 72.4, 77.1, 57.4, 73.8, 67.5, 74.2, 77.9, 51.1, 70.3, 63.4, 73.8]\ndf_lab[!, \"RESULT_Impurity\"]   = [2.5,  8.2,  4.8,  6.4,  5.2,  2.2,  6.9,  4.1,  5.6,  3.3,  2.7,  7.6,  5.4,  6.2,  3.9]\n\n# 2. Extract feature matrix and fit multi-response models\nX = Matrix{Float64}(df_lab[:, [\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"]])\nmodels = [\n  VISE_Regress_DDEF(X, df_lab[!, \"RESULT_Conversion\"], \"quadratic\"; InNames=plan.factors),\n  VISE_Regress_DDEF(X, df_lab[!, \"RESULT_Yield\"],      \"quadratic\"; InNames=plan.factors),\n  VISE_Regress_DDEF(X, df_lab[!, \"RESULT_Impurity\"],   \"quadratic\"; InNames=plan.factors)\n]\n\n# 3. Multi-threaded high-density grid exploration & DCYP\nbounds = [60.0 120.0; 5.0 25.0; 15.0 35.0]\ngoals = [\n  Dict(\"Type\" => \"Maximise\", \"Min\" => 50.0, \"Max\" => 98.0, \"Target\" => 92.0, \"Weight\" => 1.0),\n  Dict(\"Type\" => \"Maximise\", \"Min\" => 40.0, \"Max\" => 85.0, \"Target\" => 80.0, \"Weight\" => 1.2),\n  Dict(\"Type\" => \"Minimise\", \"Min\" => 1.0,  \"Max\" => 10.0, \"Target\" => 2.0,  \"Weight\" => 1.5)\n]\ndcyp = [CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES(2, log(2.0) / 109.8, \"18F\")]\n\ngrid_coords, grid_preds, grid_scores = VISE_RunGridSearch_DDEF(\n  models, goals, bounds; ModifiersDCYP=dcyp\n)\n\n# 4. Extract Candidate Portfolio (Absolute summit vs Reagent Minimisation)\nbest_idx  = argmax(grid_scores)\ntop_score = grid_scores[best_idx]\ntop_coord = grid_coords[best_idx, :]\n\ntier_mask    = grid_scores .>= (top_score * 0.90)\ntier_indices = findall(tier_mask)\nmin_prec_idx = tier_indices[argmin(grid_coords[tier_indices, 3])]\n\nprintln(\"Candidate TOP-01 (Grid Summit) : \", round.(top_coord; digits=1), \" | Score: \", round(top_score; digits=4))\nprintln(\"Candidate INP-Precursor (Green): \", round.(grid_coords[min_prec_idx, :]; digits=1), \" | Precursor: \", round(grid_coords[min_prec_idx, 3]; digits=1), \" mg\")\n\n"},{"title":"2. Multi-Threading","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#2.-Multi-Threading","category":"section","text":"The high-density grid evaluation (VISE_RunGridSearch_DDEF) scales across all available CPU cores."},{"title":"2.1. Invocation with Multiple Threads","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#2.1.-Invocation-with-Multiple-Threads","category":"section","text":"Launch Julia with automated thread detection:\n\njulia --threads=auto\n\nOr specify a dedicated thread allocation:\n\njulia --threads=8\n\nWithin Julia, verify thread availability:\n\nusing Base.Threads\nprintln(\"Allocated Threads: \", nthreads())\n\nWhen thread counts exceed 4, VISE_RunGridSearch_DDEF dynamically upgrades evaluation resolution from 21^3 (9261 coordinates) to 41^3 (68921 coordinates) without increasing execution latency.\n\n"},{"title":"3. System Image Compilation","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#3.-System-Image-Compilation","category":"section","text":"To eliminate Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation delays and achieve fast startup, compile a local system image using PackageCompiler.jl:"},{"title":"3.1. Compilation via tools/Compiler.jl","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#3.1.-Compilation-via-tools/Compiler.jl","category":"section","text":"A pre-configured compiler harness is located at tools/Compiler.jl, supporting two distinct build profiles:\n\nFull Precompilation: Compiles all dependencies alongside DoECISORY and executes the benchmark workload (tools/Workload.jl):\njulia --project=. tools/Compiler.jl\nGenerates tools/Sysimage.dll (Windows) or tools/Sysimage.so (Linux/macOS).\nDevelopment Profile (--dev): Precompiles heavy third-party dependencies (Dash, PlotlyJS, DataFrames, XLSX) while keeping DoECISORY interpreted:\njulia --project=. tools/Compiler.jl --dev\nGenerates tools/Sysimage_Deps.dll. This allows tools/Watcher.ps1 to reload code modifications instantly without requiring full sysimage re-compilation."},{"title":"3.2. Launching with Native Sysimage","page":"Scripting Guide","location":"scripting/#3.2.-Launching-with-Native-Sysimage","category":"section","text":"On Windows (PowerShell):\n\n# Standard startup\njulia -J tools\\Sysimage.dll -e \"using DoECISORY; launch()\"\n\n# Development watcher mode\njulia -J tools\\Sysimage_Deps.dll --project=. -e \"using DoECISORY; launch()\"\n\nOn Linux / macOS:\n\njulia -J tools/Sysimage.so -e \"using DoECISORY; launch()\""},{"title":"Statistical Model Diagnostics Guide","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#Statistical-Model-Diagnostics-Guide","category":"section","text":"This section explains how to interpret the diagnostic metrics, ANOVA tables, multicollinearity screening, and cross-validation indicators generated by Lib_Vise and presented in the automated analysis report.\n\n"},{"title":"1. Statistical Report Structure","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#1.-Statistical-Report-Structure","category":"section","text":"Through VISE_GenSciReport_DDEF, a structured seven-stage audit report is compiled:\n\nStage I: Design Vitals: Orthogonality, condition number, and D/A/G/I optimality metrics.\nStage II: Model Summary: R^2, Adjusted R^2, Prediction Q^2 (Cross-Validated R^2), PRESS, and AIC.\nStage III: Analysis of Variance (ANOVA): F-statistics, term p-values, and Lack-of-Fit test.\nStage IV: Regression Coefficients: Parameter estimates, standard errors, t-tests, and VIF multicollinearity screening.\nStage V: Factor Sensitivity: Normalised partial derivatives and relative factor importance ranking.\nStage VI: Multi-Objective Optimisation: Composite desirability scores and Candidate Pool ranking.\nStage VII: Kinetic & Decay Audit: Summary of precursor degradation and analyte holding latency corrections applied.\n\n"},{"title":"2. Model Fit and Validation: R^2 and Q^2","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#2.-Model-Fit-and-Validation:-R2-and-Q2","category":"section","text":"A high Coefficient of Determination (R^2) is necessary but insufficient to prove model validity. Standard polynomial regressions are susceptible to overfitting, where the fitted surface tracks experimental noise rather than underlying chemical relationships."},{"title":"2.1. Metrics Comparison","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#2.1.-Metrics-Comparison","category":"section","text":"Metric Symbol Mathematical Basis Diagnostic Role\nCoefficient of Determination R^2 1 - fracSS_textresSS_texttot Measures explained variance within the training points. Monotonically increases with added model terms.\nAdjusted R^2 R^2_textadj 1 - fracSS_textres  (N - p)SS_texttot  (N - 1) Penalises unnecessary model terms based on residual degrees of freedom.\nPrediction Q^2 Q^2 1 - fractextPRESSSS_texttot Leave-One-Out (LOO) cross-validated predictive ability across unseen data points."},{"title":"2.2. Decision Rules","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#2.2.-Decision-Rules","category":"section","text":"Robust Model: R^2  085, Q^2  070, and R^2 - Q^2  020. The model has genuine predictive capability.\nOverfitting Warning: R^2 approx 095 while Q^2  050 (or negative). Indicates that higher-order interaction or quadratic terms are modelling random experimental fluctuation. In such cases, backward term elimination or linear model simplification should be considered.\n\n"},{"title":"3. Model Sufficiency: Lack-of-Fit Testing","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#3.-Model-Sufficiency:-Lack-of-Fit-Testing","category":"section","text":"In experimental designs containing replicated centre points, the total residual sum of squares (SS_textres) is partitioned into two orthogonal components:\n\nSS_textres = SS_textpure error + SS_textlack-of-fit\n\nPure Error (SS_textpure error): Variance observed exclusively among replicate runs performed at identical factor levels.\nLack-of-Fit (SS_textlack-of-fit): Systematic divergence between the fitted mathematical surface and the true response curvature."},{"title":"3.1. Interpretation","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#3.1.-Interpretation","category":"section","text":"The Lack-of-Fit F-statistic compares mean squares:\n\nF_textLoF = fracMS_textlack-of-fitMS_textpure error\n\np_textLoF  005 (Not Significant): The model adequately captures the response surface. Systematic divergence does not exceed pure experimental noise.\np_textLoF  005 (Significant Lack-of-Fit): Indicates model inadequacy. If fitting a linear model, progression to a quadratic response surface model or inclusion of interaction terms is required.\n\n"},{"title":"4. Multicollinearity & Variance Inflation Factor (VIF)","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#4.-Multicollinearity-and-Variance-Inflation-Factor-(VIF)","category":"section","text":"Multicollinearity arises when predictor columns in the expanded model matrix are linearly correlated, inflating coefficient variances and making parameter estimates unstable.\n\nFor each model term j, DoECISORY.jl computes the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF):\n\ntextVIF_j = frac11 - R_j^2\n\nwhere R_j^2 is the coefficient of determination obtained by regressing term x_j against all remaining p-1 predictor terms."},{"title":"4.1. Analytical Guidance","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#4.1.-Analytical-Guidance","category":"section","text":"textVIF approx 10: Ideal orthogonal property (characteristic of standard Box-Behnken or Fractional designs in coded space).\n10  textVIF  50: Moderate, acceptable correlation. Standard errors remain stable.\ntextVIF  50 (or  100): Severe collinearity. Common when un-centred physical coordinates are regressed directly without coded scaling, or when severe space clipping has occurred. Recommend checking factor ranges or eliminating redundant interaction terms.\n\n"},{"title":"5. Candidate Selection Criteria","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#5.-Candidate-Selection-Criteria","category":"section","text":"When evaluating the Candidate Pool generated by VISE_RunOptimisation_DDEF, the platform presents a portfolio of viable formulations rather than enforcing an isolated mathematical summit. The decision protocol guides selection based on real-world benchtop priorities:"},{"title":"5.1. Classification Criteria","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#5.1.-Classification-Criteria","category":"section","text":"The candidate pool combines continuous metaheuristic search (BlackBoxOptim) and deterministic high-density grid evaluations, ranking up to 8 top unconstrained candidates (TOP-01 through TOP-08) alongside dedicated conservation and quality leaders:\n\nCandidate Identifier Origin & Selection Logic Primary Laboratory Objective Typical Trade-off\nTOP-01 to TOP-08 (with `` tag)* Top k le 8 ranked solutions across the combined continuous metaheuristic and dense grid search. The asterisk (*) designates the continuous BlackBoxOptim solution (e.g. TOP-01* when BBO leads, or TOP-02* if a dense grid coordinate outscores it). Uncompromised multi-objective desirability across continuous and discrete search modes. High operational setpoints; may require strict equipment precision.\nINP-Temperature Minimal factor 1 coordinate within ge 90 D_max tier. Minimise thermal stress on delicate reagents and substrates. le 10 sacrifice in composite desirability.\nINP-Time Minimal factor 2 coordinate within ge 90 D_max tier. Minimise radioactive decay loss, holding degradation, and synthesis time. Slightly narrower chemical conversion margin.\nINP-Precursor Minimal factor 3 coordinate within ge 90 D_max tier. Reagent conservation for expensive or scarce APIs. Minor shift in stoichiometric surplus.\nOUT-Conversion Maximal predicted conversion within ge 90 D_max tier. Prioritise crude radiochemical incorporation. May require slightly extended reaction duration.\nOUT-Yield Maximal predicted primary response within ge 90 D_max tier. Prioritise total isolated chemical recovery. May tolerate slightly elevated precursor consumption.\nOUT-Impurity Minimal predicted radiolytic impurity within ge 90 D_max tier. Minimise radiolytic fragments and degradation by-products for strict clinical compliance. May require slightly reduced temperature or reaction duration."},{"title":"5.2. Selection Logic","page":"Statistics","location":"statistics/#5.2.-Selection-Logic","category":"section","text":"Unconstrained Baseline (TOP-01 / TOP-01*): Inspect the global optimum. Review whether TOP-01 is the continuous metaheuristic peak (marked with *) or a top-tier discrete grid coordinate. If temperatures, reaction durations, and reagent concentrations fall comfortably inside standard operating procedures, advance with the highest-scoring candidate.\nReagent or Thermal Constraints: If precursor synthesis is challenging or the substrate is heat-sensitive, evaluate INP-Precursor or INP-Temperature. If INP-Precursor provides D ge 090 cdot D_max while reducing active precursor consumption by 30, it represents an economically and operationally superior formulation.\nPurity and Impurity Imperatives: For clinical batch release where radiolytic by-products must be strictly suppressed (e.g. impurity le 30), select OUT-Impurity.\nTransition Commitment: Feeding the selected candidate into FLOW_BuildIPKT_DDEF establishes the anchor coordinate for sequential Phase 2 design space refinement."},{"title":"Sequential Optimisation Case Study","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Sequential-Optimisation-Case-Study","category":"section","text":"This case study demonstrates the complete sequential optimisation workflow in DoECISORY.jl, tracking an experimental Fluorine-18 (^18textF) radiotracer formulation through initial recipe auditing, directional experimental design, physical decay adjustments, quadratic regression, multi-objective optimisation with kinetic penalties, and automated phase transition via ACTA and ASTM.\n\n[!NOTE] Dual-Track Documentation Architecture To bridge the gap between computational data science and benchtop laboratory practice, every step in this case study is presented in two parallel execution tracks:Track A — Julia Scripting: Direct functional API calls for automated computational pipelines, batch processing, and reproducible Pluto.jl notebooks.\nTrack B — Graphical Interface (Gui_Deck / Gui_Lens): Interactive browser-based controls, visual input cards, dropzone file ingestion, and slider widgets without writing terminal code.\n\n"},{"title":"Problem Formulation","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Problem-Formulation","category":"section","text":"Objective: Optimise synthesis and radiolabelling parameters for a Fluorine-18 (^18textF) labelled prosthetic radiotracer model.\nContinuous Factors (3):\nTemperature (X_1): 600 to 1200 ^circtextC (centre: 900 ^circtextC)\nTime (X_2): 50 to 250 textmin (centre: 150 textmin)\nPrecursor (X_3): 150 to 350 textmg (centre: 250 textmg)\nResponses (3):\nConversion (Y_1, % Radio-TLC): Maximise (Target: 92.0%, Min: 50.0%, Weight: 1.0)\nYield (Y_2, % Isolated RCY): Maximise (Target: 80.0%, Min: 40.0%, Weight: 1.2)\nImpurity (Y_3, % Radiolytic By-products): Minimise (Target: 2.0%, Max: 10.0%, Weight: 1.5)\nRadionuclide: Fluorine-18 (t_12 = 1098textmin, lambda = 0006315textmin^-1)\n\n"},{"title":"1. Recipe Specification and Stoichiometry","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#1.-Recipe-Specification-and-Stoichiometry","category":"section","text":"Before allocating radioactive doses or physical reagents, the formulation recipe is audited for mass balance, target concentration, and molar consistency."},{"title":"Track A — Julia Scripting","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-A-—-Julia-Scripting","category":"section","text":"using DoECISORY\n\n# Define formulation ingredients with discrete 3-factor bounds and mass budget\nrecipe = [\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Temperature\",   \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"L1\" => 60.0, \"L2\" => 90.0, \"L3\" => 120.0, \"Unit\" => \"degC\", \"MW\" => 0.0),\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Time\",          \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"L1\" => 5.0,  \"L2\" => 15.0, \"L3\" => 25.0,  \"Unit\" => \"min\",  \"MW\" => 0.0),\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Precursor\",     \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"L1\" => 15.0, \"L2\" => 25.0, \"L3\" => 35.0,  \"Unit\" => \"mg\",   \"MW\" => 450.5),\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Buffer_Salt\",   \"Role\" => \"Fixed\",    \"L1\" => 15.0, \"L2\" => 15.0, \"L3\" => 15.0,  \"Unit\" => \"mg\",   \"MW\" => 82.03),\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Ascorbic_Acid\", \"Role\" => \"Filler\",   \"L1\" => 0.0,  \"L2\" => 0.0,  \"L3\" => 0.0,   \"Unit\" => \"%\",    \"MW\" => 176.12)\n]\n\n# Run stoichiometric audit (1.0 mL target volume, 300.0 mM target concentration)\nbegin\n    total_vol_mL = 1.0\n    target_conc_mM = 300.0\n    audit_ok, audit_report, summary_df, total_mass_mg, _ = MOLE_AuditQuick_DDEF(recipe, total_vol_mL, target_conc_mM)\n    println(\"Stoichiometric Audit Passed: \", audit_ok)\nend"},{"title":"Track B — Graphical Interface (Gui_Deck)","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-B-—-Graphical-Interface-(Gui_Deck)","category":"section","text":"Open the browser interface at http://127.0.0.1:8060 and navigate to the Deck tab.\nUnder the Formulation Configuration card, input each reagent:\nActive Precursor (VAR): Enter Name Precursor, Role VAR, MW 450.5, Unit mg, Levels 15.0 / 25.0 / 35.0.\nProcess Variables (VAR): Add Temperature (60 / 90 / 120 °C) and Time (5 / 15 / 25 min).\nFixed Salts (FIX): Add Buffer_Salt (15.0 mg, MW 82.03).\nStabiliser (FILL): Add Ascorbic_Acid (Filler, MW 176.12).\nSet Target Volume to 1.0 mL and Target Concentration to 300 mM.\nClick Audit Mass Balance. The card updates with a green status badge (Audit Status: Passed), displaying total formulation mass (5085textmg) and molar ratios.\n\n"},{"title":"2. Design Matrix Generation (BB15)","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#2.-Design-Matrix-Generation-(BB15)","category":"section","text":"We construct a standard 15-point Box-Behnken (\"BB15\") design matrix comprising 12 edge midpoints and 3 replicated centre points, then evaluate its information efficiency."},{"title":"Track A — Julia Scripting","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-A-—-Julia-Scripting-2","category":"section","text":"# 1. Generate coded 15-run Box-Behnken design matrix in [-1, 0, 1]\nX_coded = CORE_GenDesign_DDEF(\"BB15\", 3)\n\n# 2. Evaluate D-, G-, and Condition metrics for a quadratic model\nbegin\n    metrics = CORE_CalcDesignMetrics_DDEF(Float64.(X_coded), \"quadratic\")\n    println(\"D-efficiency:     \", round(metrics[\"D\"] * 100.0, digits=2), \"%\")\n    println(\"G-efficiency:     \", round(metrics[\"G\"] * 100.0, digits=2), \"%\")\n    println(\"Condition Number: \", round(metrics[\"Condition\"], digits=2))\nend\n\n# 3. Map coded coordinates to physical laboratory units\nbegin\n    factor_config = [\n        Dict(\"Name\" => \"Temperature\", \"Levels\" => [60.0, 90.0, 120.0]),\n        Dict(\"Name\" => \"Time\",        \"Levels\" => [5.0,  15.0, 25.0]),\n        Dict(\"Name\" => \"Precursor\",   \"Levels\" => [15.0, 25.0, 35.0])\n    ]\n    X_physical = CORE_MapLevels_DDEF(X_coded, factor_config)\nend"},{"title":"Track B — Graphical Interface (Gui_Deck)","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-B-—-Graphical-Interface-(Gui_Deck)-2","category":"section","text":"In the Experimental Factors section of Deck, configure the 3 continuous factors:\nTemperature: Min 60.0, Mid 90.0, Max 120.0 (°C)\nTime: Min 5.0, Mid 15.0, Max 25.0 (min)\nPrecursor: Min 15.0, Mid 25.0, Max 35.0 (mg)\nUnder Design Geometry, select Box-Behnken (15 Runs, BB15).\nInspect the real-time Information Optimality Card: D-Efficiency (36.64%) and condition number (17.97) are displayed for the spherical 15-run domain.\nClick Download Protocol Workbook (.xlsx). The system generates and downloads DoECISORY_Protocol.xlsx, containing pre-populated benchtop execution tables.\n\n"},{"title":"3. Experimental Dataset and Analytical Observations","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#3.-Experimental-Dataset-and-Analytical-Observations","category":"section","text":"Laboratory observations are recorded across all three responses: Radio-TLC conversion (Y_1), isolated radiochemical yield (Y_2), and radio-HPLC radiolytic impurity (Y_3)."},{"title":"Track A — Julia Scripting","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-A-—-Julia-Scripting-3","category":"section","text":"begin\n    using DataFrames\n\n    # 15 Canonical experimental observations matching the Box-Behnken topology\n    df_exp = DataFrame(\n        :RUN => 1:15,\n        :VARIA_Temperature => X_physical[:, 1],\n        :VARIA_Time        => X_physical[:, 2],\n        :VARIA_Precursor   => X_physical[:, 3],\n        :RESULT_Conversion => [87.4, 53.1, 73.6, 77.2, 94.5, 87.0, 65.3, 82.7, 75.5, 92.1, 87.7, 59.9, 80.4, 70.2, 90.6],\n        :RESULT_Yield      => [77.6, 45.2, 66.1, 69.9, 72.4, 77.1, 57.4, 73.8, 67.5, 74.2, 77.9, 51.1, 70.3, 63.4, 73.8],\n        :RESULT_Impurity   => [2.5,  8.2,  4.8,  6.4,  5.2,  2.2,  6.9,  4.1,  5.6,  3.3,  2.7,  7.6,  5.4,  6.2,  3.9]\n    )\n    df_exp\nend"},{"title":"Track B — Graphical Interface (Gui_Lens)","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-B-—-Graphical-Interface-(Gui_Lens)","category":"section","text":"Following physical laboratory runs, fill the measured response values into the downloaded DoECISORY_Protocol.xlsx sheet:\nColumn RESULT_Conversion (measured % conversion).\nColumn RESULT_Yield (isolated % RCY).\nColumn RESULT_Impurity (measured % radiolytic impurity).\nSwitch to the Lens tab in your browser.\nDrag and drop DoECISORY_Protocol.xlsx into the Dataset Dropzone.\nThe system validates the table schema, detects radionuclide ^18textF, and verifies data integrity.\n\n"},{"title":"4. Regression Modelling and ANOVA","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#4.-Regression-Modelling-and-ANOVA","category":"section","text":"We fit full quadratic response surface models for all three outputs and verify goodness-of-fit, lack-of-fit sufficiency, and predictive ability."},{"title":"Track A — Julia Scripting","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-A-—-Julia-Scripting-4","category":"section","text":"begin\n    in_names  = [\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"]\n    X_mat     = Matrix{Float64}(X_physical)\n    Y_conv    = Vector{Float64}(df_exp[!, :RESULT_Conversion])\n    Y_yield   = Vector{Float64}(df_exp[!, :RESULT_Yield])\n    Y_impur   = Vector{Float64}(df_exp[!, :RESULT_Impurity])\n\n    # Fit quadratic models\n    model_conv   = VISE_Regress_DDEF(X_mat, Y_conv,   \"quadratic\"; InNames=in_names)\n    model_yield  = VISE_Regress_DDEF(X_mat, Y_yield,  \"quadratic\"; InNames=in_names)\n    model_impur  = VISE_Regress_DDEF(X_mat, Y_impur,  \"quadratic\"; InNames=in_names)\n\n    println(\"--- Model Validation Summary ---\")\n    println(\"Conversion | R²: \", round(model_conv[\"R2\"], digits=3),   \" | RMSE: \", round(model_conv[\"RMSE\"], digits=2))\n    println(\"Yield      | R²: \", round(model_yield[\"R2\"], digits=3),  \" | RMSE: \", round(model_yield[\"RMSE\"], digits=2))\n    println(\"Impurity   | R²: \", round(model_impur[\"R2\"], digits=3),  \" | RMSE: \", round(model_impur[\"RMSE\"], digits=2))\n\n    # Check ANOVA and Lack-of-Fit for Primary Response (Yield)\n    anova_yield = VISE_GenANOVATable_DDEF(model_yield, X_mat, Y_yield)\nend"},{"title":"Track B — Graphical Interface (Gui_Lens)","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-B-—-Graphical-Interface-(Gui_Lens)-2","category":"section","text":"In the Modelling Configuration panel, set Model Hierarchy to Quadratic Response Surface.\nClick Fit Models & Run Diagnostics.\nResponse metrics update instantly:\nConversion: R^2 = 0998, textRMSE = 092\nYield: R^2 = 0997, textRMSE = 090\nImpurity: R^2 = 0971, textRMSE = 054\nClick View Analytical Summary to open the 7-stage diagnostic modal:\nInspect the ANOVA table: Confirm Lack-of-Fit p-value  005 (indicating negligible model bias).\nCheck VIF values: All terms  25, confirming absence of multicollinearity.\n\n"},{"title":"5. Multi-Objective Optimisation and DCYP","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#5.-Multi-Objective-Optimisation-and-DCYP","category":"section","text":"We establish desirability criteria across all 3 responses, apply DCYP for Fluorine-18 on factor 2 (Time), and explore the multi-criteria Candidate Pool."},{"title":"Track A — Julia Scripting","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-A-—-Julia-Scripting-5","category":"section","text":"begin\n    # 1. Multi-Objective Goals\n    goals = [\n        Dict(\"Type\" => \"Maximise\", \"Min\" => 50.0, \"Max\" => 98.0, \"Target\" => 92.0, \"Weight\" => 1.0),\n        Dict(\"Type\" => \"Maximise\", \"Min\" => 40.0, \"Max\" => 85.0, \"Target\" => 80.0, \"Weight\" => 1.2),\n        Dict(\"Type\" => \"Minimise\", \"Min\" => 1.0,  \"Max\" => 10.0, \"Target\" => 2.0,  \"Weight\" => 1.5)\n    ]\n\n    # 2. DCYP Modifier: λ = ln(2) / 109.8 on Factor 2 (Time)\n    lambda_f18 = log(2.0) / 109.8\n    dcyp_mod   = CORE_ModifierDCYP_DDES(2, lambda_f18, \"18F\")\n\n    # 3. Continuous Optimisation & Candidate Pool Generation\n    bounds = hcat(minimum(X_mat; dims=1)', maximum(X_mat; dims=1)')\n    models = [model_conv, model_yield, model_impur]\n\n    best_coords, best_score = CORE_OptimiseDesirability_DDEF(\n        models, goals, bounds; \n        MaxTime=0.4, \n        ModifiersDCYP=[dcyp_mod]\n    )\n\n    opt_preds     = [round(VISE_Predict_DDEF(m, reshape(best_coords, 1, 3))[1]; digits=1) for m in models]\n    sensitivities = VISE_AnalyseSensitivity_DDEF(models[1], best_coords, X_mat)\n\n    println(\"\\n--- Global Optimum (TOP-01*) ---\")\n    println(\"  Temperature:   \", round(best_coords[1], digits=2), \" °C\")\n    println(\"  Reaction Time: \", round(best_coords[2], digits=2), \" min (Decay-penalised)\")\n    println(\"  Precursor:     \", round(best_coords[3], digits=2), \" mg\")\n    println(\"  Composite Desirability: \", round(best_score, digits=4))\n    println(\"  Predicted Responses:    \", opt_preds)\n    println(\"  Factor Sensitivities:   \", round.(sensitivities .* 100.0, digits=1), \"%\")\nend"},{"title":"Track B — Graphical Interface (Gui_Lens)","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-B-—-Graphical-Interface-(Gui_Lens)-3","category":"section","text":"In the Desirability Optimization card, configure criteria:\nConversion: Type Maximise, Min 50.0, Max 98.0, Target 92.0, Weight 1.0\nYield: Type Maximise, Min 40.0, Max 85.0, Target 80.0, Weight 1.2\nImpurity: Type Minimise, Min 1.0, Max 10.0, Target 2.0, Weight 1.5\nToggle Enable DCYP Kinetic Penalty:\nTarget Factor: Time\nHalf-Life Preset: Select Fluorine-18 (109.8 min)\nClick Execute Optimization & Generate Candidate Pool.\nThe interactive 3D Plotly response surface updates with the optimal coordinates, while the Candidate Portfolio Table displays all extracted compromises:\n\nCandidate ID Temp (°C) Time (min) Precursor (mg) Pred. Conv (%) Pred. Yield (%) Pred. Impurity (%) Desirability Operational Rationale\nTOP-01* 103.2 15.5 27.8 92.0 79.6 2.1 0.8970 Unconstrained Global Summit\nTOP-02 102.0 16.0 28.0 91.8 79.4 2.2 0.8961 High-Density Grid Mode\nINP-Precursor 105.0 15.0 20.0 90.8 78.1 2.5 0.8415 28% Precursor API savings\nOUT-Impurity 100.0 14.5 30.0 91.5 79.2 1.8 0.8842 Minimal radiolytic impurity release\n\nDecision: While INP-Precursor offers valuable precursor conservation, the clinical production campaign prioritises maximum radiochemical yield and minimal impurity generation. The team designates TOP-01* ([103.18, 15.50, 27.80]) as the active leader. In the UI, select the radio button beside TOP-01*.\n\n"},{"title":"6. Sequential Phase Transition (ACTA and ASTM)","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#6.-Sequential-Phase-Transition-(ACTA-and-ASTM)","category":"section","text":"The chosen leader is advanced into Phase 2 using the two-stage IPKT framework: ACTA adapts continuous boundaries around the leader coordinate, while ASTM applies affine coordinate transformation and safety clamping."},{"title":"Track A — Julia Scripting","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-A-—-Julia-Scripting-6","category":"section","text":"begin\n    # Selected Leader Coordinates: TOP-01*\n    leader_coords = [103.18, 15.50, 27.80]\n\n    # Previous Phase 1 Boundaries [Min, Mid, Max]\n    old_levels = [\n        [60.0, 90.0, 120.0], # Factor 1: Temperature\n        [5.0,  15.0, 25.0],  # Factor 2: Time\n        [15.0, 25.0, 35.0]   # Factor 3: Precursor\n    ]\n\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Stage 1: ACTA Boundary Adaptation (p_i evaluation, contraction c = 0.50)\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    acta_levels = Vector{Vector{Float64}}(undef, 3)\n    for i in 1:3\n        # FLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF(LeaderVal, OldLevels, ContractionFactor, TranslationFactor, MinLimit)\n        acta_levels[i] = FLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF(leader_coords[i], old_levels[i], 0.50, 0.0, 0.0)\n    end\n\n    println(\"--- Stage 1: ACTA Adapted Factor Bounds ---\")\n    println(\"Factor 1 (Temp)   Levels: \", round.(acta_levels[1], digits=2), \" °C\")\n    println(\"Factor 2 (Time)   Levels: \", round.(acta_levels[2], digits=2), \" min\")\n    println(\"Factor 3 (Precur) Levels: \", round.(acta_levels[3], digits=2), \" mg\")\n\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Stage 2: ASTM Affine Transformation & Clamping (x_new = α · x_old + β)\n    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Temperature: Thermodynamic affine scaling (α = 0.90, β = 9.0)\n    temp_astm = [FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF(x, 0.90, 9.0) for x in acta_levels[1]]\n\n    # Reaction Time: Direct preservation (α = 1.0, β = 0.0)\n    time_astm = [FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF(x, 1.0, 0.0) for x in acta_levels[2]]\n\n    # Precursor: Systematic Offset + Absolute Safety Clamping [10.0, 50.0] mg\n    prec_astm = [FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF(x, 1.0, 0.0) for x in acta_levels[3]]\n    prec_phase2 = [FLOW_ValidateASTM_DDEF(x, 10.0, 50.0)[2] for x in prec_astm]\n\n    println(\"\\n--- Stage 2: ASTM Transformed Phase 2 Operational Space ---\")\n    println(\"Phase 2 Temperature Range : [\", round(temp_astm[1], digits=2), \", \", round(temp_astm[3], digits=2), \"] °C\")\n    println(\"Phase 2 Reaction Time Range: [\", round(time_astm[1], digits=2), \", \", round(time_astm[3], digits=2), \"] min\")\n    println(\"Phase 2 Precursor Range    : [\", round(prec_phase2[1], digits=2), \", \", round(prec_phase2[3], digits=2), \"] mg\")\nend"},{"title":"Track B — Graphical Interface (Gui_Lens → Gui_Deck)","page":"Case Study","location":"tutorial/#Track-B-—-Graphical-Interface-(Gui_Lens-Gui_Deck)","category":"section","text":"In the Interphase Knowledge Transfer (IPKT) panel of Lens:\nConfirm the active leader is set to TOP-01*.\nAdjust the Domain Contraction Ratio (c) slider to 0.50 (narrowing the search window by 50 centered on x^*).\nIn the ASTM Transformation Settings:\nSet Temperature scaling alpha = 090, offset beta = 90 °C.\nSet Reaction Time scaling alpha = 10, offset beta = 00.\nSet Precursor limits: Absolute Min 10.0, Absolute Max 50.0 mg.\nClick Transfer Knowledge to Phase 2.\nThe application automatically commits the IPKT payload via FLOW_CommitIPKT_DDEF and transitions to the Deck workspace:\nThe new Phase 2 factor bounds are immediately populated into the formulation cards.\nPrior mathematical variance and historical coefficients are preserved.\nClick Download Protocol Workbook (.xlsx) to download the Phase 2 experimental protocol ready for laboratory execution."},{"title":"Citation & Licence","page":"Citation","location":"citation/#Citation-and-Licence","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Academic Citation","page":"Citation","location":"citation/#Academic-Citation","category":"section","text":"If you use DoECISORY.jl in your research or publications, please cite the software as follows: \n\n@software{Gol_DoECISORY_2026,\n  author       = {Göl, Eren Selim},\n  title        = {{DoECISORY.jl: Design of Experiments with Computational Interactive Sequential Optimisation for Response Yield}},\n  version      = {0.2.0},\n  year         = {2026},\n  month        = {10},\n  publisher    = {Zenodo},\n  doi          = {10.5281/zenodo.23276841},\n  url          = {https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23276841}\n}"},{"title":"Author & Contact","page":"Citation","location":"citation/#Author-and-Contact","category":"section","text":"Eren Selim GÖL, MPharm   Radiopharmacy Researcher | Lead Software Architect & Developer  \n\nDepartment of Radiopharmacy, Faculty of Pharmacy, Hacettepe University  \nORCID: 0009-0000-4491-7759  \nlinkedin.com/in/erensgol\n\n"},{"title":"Software Licence","page":"Citation","location":"citation/#Software-Licence","category":"section","text":"DoECISORY.jl is open-source software licensed under the Mozilla Public License 2.0 (MPL-2.0)."},{"title":"Visual Decision Analytics Guide","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#Visual-Decision-Analytics-Guide","category":"section","text":"This guide details the scientific visualisation and graphic decision analytics implemented in DoECISORY.jl via Lib_Arts and PlotlyJS. \n\nVisual analytics in DoECISORY.jl serve a dual purpose: enabling laboratory researchers to interactively explore multidimensional response landscapes in the browser interface, and generating publication-grade vector graphics for peer-reviewed academic manuscripts.\n\n"},{"title":"1. Visual Standards & Design System","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#1.-Visual-Standards-and-Design-System","category":"section","text":"All graphic routines in DoECISORY.jl adhere to strict biostatistical visual standards:\n\nScientific Colour Mapping (Viridis Palette):  Response surfaces, contour gradients, and 3D heatmaps natively use the Viridis colour scale. Viridis offers three vital properties for peer-reviewed literature:\nPerceptual Uniformity: Equal steps in numerical data correspond to equal steps in perceived brightness, preventing artificial visual banding or pseudo-features.\nAccessibility: Remains fully legible for individuals with red-green (deuteranopia, protanopia) or blue-yellow (tritanopia) colour vision deficiencies.\nMonochrome Reproduction: Converts smoothly to linear greyscale when printed in black-and-white journals without loss of topographic depth.\nTypographic Hierarchy & Contrast: Minimalist grid layouts, zero-offset axis lines, and high-contrast marker symbology designed for clarity at manuscript column widths (85textmm single column or 175textmm double column).\nDual Rendering Targets:\nInteractive Web Client (Gui_Lens): High frame-rate WebGL surface meshes with 6-degrees-of-freedom camera rotation, slice sliders, and contextual hover tooltips.\nScripted Vector Export (PlotlyJS.savefig): Lossless vector export (.svg, .pdf) and high-resolution raster export (.png at ge 300textDPI).\n\n"},{"title":"2. Response Surface Topography & 3-Factor Slicing","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#2.-Response-Surface-Topography-and-3-Factor-Slicing","category":"section","text":"Polynomial response models capture complex non-linear curvatures, but human visual perception is inherently constrained to three spatial dimensions (two input factors against one output response).\n\n   Factor X2 (Time)\n       ▲\n       │        ┌───────────────────────────────┐\n  Max  │        │   Contour Projection (2D)     │\n       │        │   High conversion plateau     │\n  Mid  │        │   [Equipotential isoclines]   │\n       │        │                               │\n  Min  │        └───────────────────────────────┘\n       └──────────────────────────────────────────► Factor X1 (Temperature)\n               Min            Mid            Max\n                               ▲\n                               │ [Pin Factor X3 (Precursor) via Slicing]"},{"title":"2.1. 3D Response Surfaces (ARTS_RenderSurface_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#2.1.-3D-Response-Surfaces-(ARTS_RenderSurface_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"The 3D response surface visualises the predicted mathematical manifold haty = f(x_1 x_2) across the active design space.\n\nusing DoECISORY, PlotlyJS\n\n# Renders a 3D response surface for Yield across Temperature (X1) and Time (X2)\n# while fixing Precursor (X3) at its central coordinate (0.0 in coded space)\nfig_surface = ARTS_RenderSurface_DDEF(\n    model_yield, \n    1, 2;               # Factor indices for X and Y axes\n    SliceFactor=3, \n    SliceVal=0.0,       # Coded coordinate for pinned 3rd factor\n    InNames=[\"Temperature (°C)\", \"Time (min)\", \"Precursor (mg)\"],\n    OutName=\"Radiochemical Yield (%)\"\n)\n\nDiagnostic Interpretation:\n\nGlobal Peak / Dome: Signifies a well-behaved quadratic maximum lying within the search domain.\nRidge / Rising Ridge: Indicates that factors act cooperatively; the true physical summit may reside outside current boundaries, signaling the need for sequential translation (ACTA).\nSaddle Point (Minimax): Represents antagonistic cross-term interaction (x_1 x_2), where moving in one direction increases response while moving orthogonally decreases it."},{"title":"2.2. 2D Equipotential Contour Projections (ARTS_RenderContour_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#2.2.-2D-Equipotential-Contour-Projections-(ARTS_RenderContour_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"Contour maps project the 3D surface onto a 2D plane, displaying lines of constant response (isoclines).\n\nfig_contour = ARTS_RenderContour_DDEF(\n    model_yield, \n    1, 2; \n    SliceFactor=3, \n    SliceVal=0.0,\n    InNames=[\"Temperature (°C)\", \"Time (min)\", \"Precursor (mg)\"],\n    OutName=\"Radiochemical Yield (%)\"\n)\n\nDiagnostic Interpretation:\n\nElliptical contours indicate strong synergistic or antagonistic two-factor interaction terms (beta_ij x_i x_j).\nCircular or concentric contours indicate independent linear and pure quadratic contributions without significant cross-product distortion."},{"title":"2.3. Orthogonal 3-Factor Slicing (ARTS_RenderSlice_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#2.3.-Orthogonal-3-Factor-Slicing-(ARTS_RenderSlice_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"To systematically explore the full 3-factor volume without loss of information, ARTS_RenderSlice_DDEF evaluates cross-sectional slices across the third factor:\n\n# Evaluates slices at Lower (-1.0), Centre (0.0), and Upper (+1.0) levels\nfig_slices = ARTS_RenderSlice_DDEF(\n    model_yield, \n    1, 2, 3; \n    SliceLevels=[-1.0, 0.0, 1.0],\n    InNames=[\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"],\n    OutName=\"Yield (%)\"\n)\n\nIn the Lens web interface, this slicing is controlled interactively via the Factor Slice Slider, allowing researchers to glide smoothly along the third dimension and observe the expansion or contraction of the optimal zone.\n\n"},{"title":"3. Statistical Diagnostic Graphics (Model Validation)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#3.-Statistical-Diagnostic-Graphics-(Model-Validation)","category":"section","text":"Before drawing physical conclusions from response surfaces, the underlying Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression must be validated using diagnostic plots.\n\n   Standardised Effect |t|\n       ▲\n       │   █\n       │   █   █\n       │   █   █   █\n  t_crit ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─  [p = 0.05 Significance Line]\n       │   █   █   █   █   █\n       │   █   █   █   █   █   █   █\n       └──────────────────────────────────► Model Term\n           X1  X2 X1² X2² X1*X2 X3  X3²"},{"title":"3.1. Pareto Chart of Standardised Effects (ARTS_RenderPareto_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#3.1.-Pareto-Chart-of-Standardised-Effects-(ARTS_RenderPareto_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"The Pareto chart ranks the absolute magnitude of studentised regression coefficients (t_j = hatbeta_j  textSE(hatbeta_j)) in descending order:\n\nfig_pareto = ARTS_RenderPareto_DDEF(\n    model_yield; \n    Alpha=0.05, \n    Title=\"Pareto Chart of Standardised Effects (Yield)\"\n)\n\nReference Line (p = 005): Any effect exceeding the red vertical threshold line is statistically significant at the 95% confidence level.\nModel Reduction Guide: Factors and interactions falling well below the critical line represent negligible contributors and can be considered for hierarchical term elimination."},{"title":"3.2. Observed vs. Predicted Response Fit (ARTS_RenderFit_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#3.2.-Observed-vs.-Predicted-Response-Fit-(ARTS_RenderFit_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"Juxtaposes experimental observations against fitted model predictions along the y = x diagonal identity line:\n\nfig_fit = ARTS_RenderFit_DDEF(\n    model_yield; \n    OutName=\"Radiochemical Yield (%)\"\n)\n\nTight Clustering along Diagonal: Confirms high coefficient of determination (R^2) and minimal unexplained residual variance.\nSystematic Curvature: Points sagging below the diagonal at low values and bowing above at high values indicate missing quadratic or interaction terms."},{"title":"3.3. Residual Diagnostics (ARTS_RenderResiduals_DDEF & ARTS_RenderQQ_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#3.3.-Residual-Diagnostics-(ARTS_RenderResiduals_DDEF-and-ARTS_RenderQQ_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"Evaluates the core Gauss-Markov assumptions of Ordinary Least Squares regression:\n\n# 1. Residuals vs Predicted (Homoscedasticity)\nfig_residuals = ARTS_RenderResiduals_DDEF(model_yield)\n\n# 2. Normal Q-Q Plot of Residuals (Normality)\nfig_qq = ARTS_RenderQQ_DDEF(model_yield)\n\nHomoscedasticity Check: Residuals should scatter randomly within a constant horizontal band around zero. A funnel or wedge shape indicates heteroscedasticity (variance depending on response magnitude), suggesting a power or logarithmic response transformation.\nNormality Check: Studentised residuals in the Normal Q-Q plot should align tightly along the straight theoretical quantile reference line. S-shaped departures indicate heavy-tailed distributions or outliers."},{"title":"3.4. Factor Sensitivity & Perturbation (ARTS_RenderSensitivity_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#3.4.-Factor-Sensitivity-and-Perturbation-(ARTS_RenderSensitivity_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"Compares the relative responsiveness of all input factors radiating outwards from a designated reference coordinate (typically the design centre or active leader coordinate):\n\nfig_sens = ARTS_RenderSensitivity_DDEF(\n    model_yield, \n    [90.0, 15.0, 25.0]; # Reference operating point [Temperature, Time, Precursor]\n    InNames=[\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"]\n)\n\nSteeper curves denote factors with higher sensitivity, where small physical departures cause sharp response changes.\nFlat lines denote insensitive or robust factors, suitable for cost-saving adjustments or wider equipment tolerances.\n\n"},{"title":"4. Multi-Response Sweet-Spot Zones & Candidate Portfolios","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#4.-Multi-Response-Sweet-Spot-Zones-and-Candidate-Portfolios","category":"section","text":"When balancing conflicting laboratory criteria (e.g. maximising yield while simultaneously suppressing impurity formation), isolated single-response plots are insufficient."},{"title":"4.1. Three-Tier Decision Space Architecture (PLATE60)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#4.1.-Three-Tier-Decision-Space-Architecture-(PLATE60)","category":"section","text":"Rather than relying on a single arbitrary cutoff, DoECISORY.jl structures the multi-criteria decision space into three concentric operating tiers:\n\nFeasible Space (ARTS_RenderFeasibleSpace_DDEF): Demarcates the broad permissible region where all individual response specifications are satisfied (D_i ge 005). This forms the baseline regulatory compliance perimeter.\nOptimal Zone (ARTS_RenderOptimalZone_DDEF): Restricts the operating space to coordinates achieving at least 90 of the maximum composite desirability (D ge 090 times D_max). Serves as the primary operational territory for process engineering.\nSweet Spot (ARTS_RenderSweetSpot_DDEF): Isolates the core operating nucleus within 5 tolerance of summit desirability (D ge 095 times D_max). This provides the target setpoint coordinates for validated clinical batch release.\n\n# 1. Feasible Space (Baseline Compliance: D >= 0.05)\nfig_feasible = ARTS_RenderFeasibleSpace_DDEF(models, goals, 1, 2; SliceFactor=3, SliceVal=0.0)\n\n# 2. Optimal Zone (High Yield: D >= 0.90 * D_max)\nfig_optimal  = ARTS_RenderOptimalZone_DDEF(models, goals, 1, 2; SliceFactor=3, SliceVal=0.0)\n\n# 3. Sweet Spot (Primary Operating Nucleus: D >= 0.95 * D_max)\nfig_sweet    = ARTS_RenderSweetSpot_DDEF(models, goals, 1, 2; SliceFactor=3, SliceVal=0.0)\n\nThe concentric projection visually demonstrates how parameter tolerance tightens from broad feasibility towards the optimum."},{"title":"4.2. Candidate Portfolio Spatial Mapping (ARTS_RenderCandidates_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#4.2.-Candidate-Portfolio-Spatial-Mapping-(ARTS_RenderCandidates_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"Visualises where the extracted candidates from the multi-criteria portfolio sit within the 3-factor parameter space:\n\nfig_candidates = ARTS_RenderCandidates_DDEF(\n    candidates_dict, # Generated via FLOW_GetCandidates_DDEF\n    in_names\n)\n\nTOP-01 to TOP-08: Clustered near the global desirability plateau.\nINP-<Factor>: Positioned along the low-consumption boundary edges (e.g. INP-Precursor), visually demonstrating the input savings achieved relative to the global peak.\nOUT-<Response>: Oriented towards the optimal frontier for specific critical quality attributes (e.g. OUT-Impurity).\n\n"},{"title":"5. Sequential Interphase Dynamics (IPKT Interval Migration)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#5.-Sequential-Interphase-Dynamics-(IPKT-Interval-Migration)","category":"section","text":"A core methodological strength of DoECISORY.jl is the seamless transition of experimental knowledge between successive experimental phases without loss of historical variance or coordinate fidelity.\n\nIn laboratory investigations, sequential optimisation requires moving from a broad initial exploration space (Phase 1) to a focused, high-precision domain (Phase 2). The Inter-Phase Knowledge Transfer (IPKT) framework automates this transition via two complementary mathematical engines:\n\nAdaptive Contraction & Translation Algorithm (ACTA): Governs intra-system factor range adaptation, computing continuous coordinate adjustments, domain narrowing, gradient-directed translations, and physical boundary clamping.\nAffine Space Transformation Model (ASTM): Governs cross-system mappings and scale-up projections (e.g. mapping coordinates from a benchtop micro-vial to an automated cassette synthesiser).\n\n"},{"title":"5.1. Visual Canvas Architecture (FLOW_RenderIPKT_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#5.1.-Visual-Canvas-Architecture-(FLOW_RenderIPKT_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"The IPKT transition chart visualises how factor domains shift, narrow, or translate relative to their previous baseline. To allow simultaneous visual comparison across disparate physical dimensions (e.g. Temperature in °C, Time in min, and Precursor in mg), FLOW_RenderIPKT_DDEF projects all variables onto a normalised dimensionless axis spanning -20 +20:\n\n                          IPKT Multi-Layer Visual Trace Canvas\n                          \n  Exploration Floor                                                        Exploration Ceiling\n  (Safety Fence: -2.0)                                                     (Safety Fence: +2.0)\n          │                                                                        │\n          ▼                                                                        ▼\n   -2.0   │   -1.5        -1.0 (Lower)     -0.5          0.0         +0.5       +1.0 (Upper)    +1.5       +2.0\n────┆─────┼─────┼──────────────┼─────────────┼───────────┼────────────┼──────────────┼────────────┼─────────┆────\n    ┆     │                    │                                                     │            │         ┆\n    ┆     │                    60.0 [Phase 1 Lower]                120.0 [Phase 1 Upper]          │         ┆\n    ┆     │                    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │         ┆\n    ┆     │   Trace 1: Current │░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░│ (Dark Gray)│         ┆\n    ┆     │                    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │         ┆\n    ┆     │                                                 ┌────────────────────────┐            │         ┆\n    ┆     │   Trace 2: Target                               │█████████████●██████████│ (Gold Bar) │         ┆\n    ┆     │                                                 └─────────────┼──────────┘            │         ┆\n    ┆     │                                                               │                       │         ┆\n    ┆     │                                                Trace 3: Target Centre (Green Dot)     │         ┆\n    ┆     │                                                Trace 4: Leader Point (Magenta Dot)    │         ┆\n    ┆     │                                                               │                       │         ┆\n    ┆     │                                            88.18            103.18         118.18     │         ┆\n    ┆     │                                           [P2 Min]        [P2 Centre]     [P2 Max]    │         ┆\n────┆─────┴─────┼──────────────┼─────────────┼───────────┼────────────┼──────────────┼────────────┴─────────┆────\n    ▲                                                                                                       ▲\n    └──────────────── Red Dashed Exploration Limit Lines (Hard Boundary Warning) ───────────────────────────┘"},{"title":"Canvas Components & Traces:","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#Canvas-Components-and-Traces:","category":"section","text":"Trace 1: Current Baseline (Dark Slate Gray, FD.COLOUR_DARLOW): Fixed horizontal bar of width 12 spanning exactly -10 +10, representing the normalised boundaries of the previous Phase 1 operational window L_textold U_textold.\nTrace 2: Target Domain (Vibrant Gold, FD.COLOUR_HUEYEL): Dynamic horizontal bar of width 12 spanning n_textnewmin n_textnewmax, indicating the proposed Phase 2 operational range.\nTrace 3: Target Centre (Emerald Green Circle, FD.COLOUR_TONGRE): Marker placed at n_textnewmid, pinpointing the geometric centre (zero-level, 0) of the proposed Phase 2 design.\nTrace 4: Optimal Leader Point (Magenta Circle, FD.COLOUR_SHAMAG): Marker placed at n_textleader, indicating the exact physical coordinate of the champion formulation selected from Phase 1.\nTrace 5: Directional Axial Focus (Teal Circle, #21918C): Visible when using directional screening geometries (such as DF14), highlighting the primary exploratory axial direction (Lower -1 or Upper +1).\nDual Numeric Level Annotations:\nTop Labels (y + 035): Low-contrast gray numerals positioned at -10 and +10 displaying the previous Phase 1 physical boundaries.\nBottom Labels (y - 035): High-contrast bold numerals positioned at n_textnewmin and n_textnewmax displaying the adapted Phase 2 physical boundaries.\nExploration Safety Fences (Red Dashed Vertical Lines at pm 20): Demarcate the maximum allowable extrapolation range; any target boundary approaching pm 20 triggers a visual boundary warning.\n\n"},{"title":"5.2. ACTA Adaptation Signatures","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#5.2.-ACTA-Adaptation-Signatures","category":"section","text":"The ACTA engine dynamically selects an adaptation strategy based on the spatial location of the optimal leader coordinate x^* relative to the previous factor domain L_textold U_textold (width Delta x = U_textold - L_textold). The tolerance threshold is set to tau = 005 cdot Delta x (5% safety margin from either boundary).\n\n   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │                                ACTA Decision Topology                                  │\n   ├───────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┤\n   │ Leader Position       │ Boundary Trait                │ Recommended Adaptation         │\n   ├───────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n   │ x* ∈ [L + τ, U - τ]   │ FLOW_BoundarySafe_DDES        │ Contraction (c ∈ (0.0, 1.0])   │\n   │ x* < L + τ            │ FLOW_BoundaryLower_DDES       │ Translation Downward (δ < 0.0) │\n   │ x* > U - τ            │ FLOW_BoundaryUpper_DDES       │ Translation Upward (δ > 0.0)   │\n   └───────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘"},{"title":"1. Internal Centering & Contraction (FLOW_BoundarySafe_DDES)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#1.-Internal-Centering-and-Contraction-(FLOW_BoundarySafe_DDES)","category":"section","text":"Mathematical Condition: The leader point lies comfortably inside the design space (L_textold + tau le x^* le U_textold - tau).\nAdaptation Mechanism: The search span is contracted symmetrically around the leader:\nDelta x_textnew = c cdot Delta x_textold quad x_textmid = x^*\nL_textnew = x_textmid - fracDelta x_textnew2 quad U_textnew = x_textmid + fracDelta x_textnew2\n(Default contraction factor c = 050, focusing variance on the optimum).\nVisual Signature: The gold target bar is narrower than the gray baseline bar and rests entirely inside -10 +10. The magenta leader dot and green centre dot align at the midpoint of the gold bar."},{"title":"2. Boundary Proximity & Directional Translation (FLOW_BoundaryLower_DDES / Upper_DDES)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#2.-Boundary-Proximity-and-Directional-Translation-(FLOW_BoundaryLower_DDES-/-Upper_DDES)","category":"section","text":"Mathematical Condition: The leader point is located within 5% of a boundary limit (x^*  L_textold + tau or x^*  U_textold - tau), indicating that the true optimum lies outside the current exploration space.\nAdaptation Mechanism: Contraction is suspended (Delta x_textnew = Delta x_textold) to prevent premature narrowing in an uncharted region. Instead, the entire domain is translated along the response gradient by translation factor delta in -10 10:\nx_textmid = x^* + delta cdot fracDelta x_textnew2\nVisual Signature: The gold bar shifts into the outer quadrants (-20 -10 or +10 +20). The magenta leader dot sits near the boundary edge, visually demonstrating that Phase 2 will explore new physical territory."},{"title":"3. Safety Clamping & Physical Ceilings","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#3.-Safety-Clamping-and-Physical-Ceilings","category":"section","text":"Mathematical Condition: The calculated bounds violate immutable physical or equipment boundaries (e.g. solvent boiling point T le 90^circtextC, non-negative concentrations C ge 00, or pump delivery limits).\nAdaptation Mechanism: ACTA enforces hard boundary clamping:\nL_textnew = max(L^textabs L_textnew) quad U_textnew = min(U^textabs U_textnew)\nVisual Signature: The gold target bar terminates abruptly at the constraint threshold. The bottom numeric label reflects the physical clamp rather than an unconstrained mathematical projection."},{"title":"4. Variable Replacement (Screening Transition)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#4.-Variable-Replacement-(Screening-Transition)","category":"section","text":"Mathematical Condition: When transitioning from a coarse screening phase to a fine response surface phase, an uninfluential factor (flat response slope) is dropped and replaced with a newly identified critical process parameter.\nVisual Signature: When is_replaced = true, the baseline numeric labels display — (em-dash), and the target bar renders a fresh -10 +10 domain for the incoming factor.\n\n"},{"title":"5.3. Programmatic Transition Workflow","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#5.3.-Programmatic-Transition-Workflow","category":"section","text":"The following script illustrates an end-to-end IPKT diagnostic workflow in Julia, showing how FLOW_BuildIPKT_DDEF extracts historical candidates and generates the interactive Plotly visual specification via FLOW_RenderIPKT_DDEF:\n\nusing DoECISORY, PlotlyJS\n\n# 1. Define Phase 1 configuration (18F Baseline)\nold_config = [\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Temperature\", \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"Levels\" => [60.0, 90.0, 120.0]),\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Time\",        \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"Levels\" => [5.0,  15.0, 25.0]),\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Precursor\",   \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"Levels\" => [15.0, 25.0, 35.0])\n]\n\n# 2. Champion leader coordinates extracted from Phase 1 optimisation (TOP-01*)\n# Temperature: 103.18°C (Safe, internal)\n# Time: 15.50 min (Safe, internal, decay-penalised)\n# Precursor: 27.80 mg (Safe, internal)\nleader_coords = [103.18, 15.50, 27.80]\n\n# 3. Compute Phase 2 adapted levels via ACTA (Contraction c = 0.50, Translation δ = 0.0)\nnew_config = [\n    # Temperature: Symmetrically contracted around 103.18°C\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Temperature\", \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"Levels\" => [88.18, 103.18, 118.18]),\n    # Time: Symmetrically contracted around 15.50 min\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Time\",        \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"Levels\" => [10.50, 15.50, 20.50]),\n    # Precursor: Symmetrically contracted around 27.80 mg\n    Dict(\"Name\" => \"Precursor\",   \"Role\" => \"Variable\", \"Levels\" => [22.80, 27.80, 32.80])\n]\n\n# 4. Generate Interactive IPKT Diagnostic Plot\nfig_ipkt_dict = FLOW_RenderIPKT_DDEF(\n    old_config, \n    new_config, \n    leader_coords;\n    Method=\"BB15\"\n)\n\n# Render via PlotlyJS\np_ipkt = PlotlyJS.Plot(fig_ipkt_dict[\"data\"], fig_ipkt_dict[\"layout\"])\nPlotlyJS.savefig(p_ipkt, \"Figure_IPKT_Transition.pdf\")\n\n"},{"title":"5.4. Laboratory Pre-Commit Verification Protocol","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#5.4.-Laboratory-Pre-Commit-Verification-Protocol","category":"section","text":"Prior to clicking Commit Phase 2 in the Graphical Interface (Gui_Deck) or calling FLOW_CommitIPKT_DDEF via script, the experimental team should verify the following visual criteria on the IPKT diagnostic canvas:\n\nDiagnostic Feature Visual Cue Verification Rule Action Required if Unmet\nCentering Check Green Dot vs Gold Bar The green circle must sit at the exact midpoint of the gold target bar. If off-centre, check whether an intentional translation factor (delta ne 0) was entered.\nBoundary Margin Magenta Dot vs Gray Edges Verify whether the magenta dot lies within the central 90% of the dark gray bar. If touching or near -10 or +10, activate Translation (delta) to shift search space into unchartered domain.\nClamping Verification Bar End vs Physical Cap Confirm that gold bars do not exceed solvent boiling point, freezing point, or pump limits. If clamped, verify that the experimental design does not produce unattainable process set-points.\nExtrapolation Fence Gold Bar vs Red Lines (pm 20) No portion of the gold bar should cross beyond the red dashed fences at pm 20. If breaching pm 20, reduce translation step delta to prevent dangerous extrapolation away from verified data.\nFactor Continuity Top vs Bottom Variable Names Ensure all active factors match intended designations; screened variables should show —. Verify sheet configuration if factor roles or names have inadvertently swapped.\n\n"},{"title":"6. Programmatic Export & 145 mm Composite Plates","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#6.-Programmatic-Export-and-145-mm-Composite-Plates","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"6.1. Publication-Ready 145 mm Composite Vector Plates (FAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF)","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#6.1.-Publication-Ready-145-mm-Composite-Vector-Plates-(FAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF)","category":"section","text":"For academic manuscripts, doctoral dissertations, and technical reports, DoECISORY.jl standardises graphic exports onto 145 mm fixed-width composite SVG plates (PLATE11 to PLATE60). Rather than manually stitching disparate vector outputs in third-party graphic editors, FAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF dynamically combines individual panels into cohesive multi-panel plates:\n\n2×3 Multi-Panel Grids (115200textmm height, e.g. 2D contour pairs, slices, and diagnostic panels)\n3×3 Manifold Matrices (172800textmm height, e.g. interaction matrices and response surfaces)\n1×3 Linear Strips (57600textmm height, e.g. isolated factor progressions)\n\nusing DoECISORY\n\n# Assemble individual vector panel strings into a standardised 145 mm 2x3 plate\nsvg_plate = FAST_AssembleSvgPlate_DDEF(panel_svg_strings, :Grid2x3)\nwrite(\"PLATE11_Contour_Slices.svg\", svg_plate)\n\nEach composite plate features:\n\nStandardised 8 pt bold panel labels ((a), (b), etc.) for direct manuscript citation,\nStrict physical scaling calibrated to standard two-column page widths without text distortion."},{"title":"6.2. Direct PlotlyJS Single-Plot Export","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#6.2.-Direct-PlotlyJS-Single-Plot-Export","category":"section","text":"Individual PlotlyJS.Plot objects returned by Lib_Arts can also be exported directly into lossless vector or high-resolution raster formats:\n\nusing PlotlyJS\n\n# 1. Lossless Vector Export for Journal Submissions (PDF / SVG)\nPlotlyJS.savefig(fig_surface, \"Figure_3_Surface.pdf\"; width=800, height=600)\nPlotlyJS.savefig(fig_pareto,  \"Figure_4_Pareto.svg\";  width=600, height=450)\n\n# 2. High-Resolution Raster Export (300+ DPI PNG)\nPlotlyJS.savefig(fig_contour, \"Figure_5_Contour.png\"; width=1200, height=900, scale=3)\n\n# 3. Standalone Interactive HTML (Supplementary Information)\nPlotlyJS.savefig(fig_surface, \"Supplementary_3D_Surface.html\")"},{"title":"6.3. Automated Portfolio ZIP Archive","page":"Visualisations","location":"visuals/#6.3.-Automated-Portfolio-ZIP-Archive","category":"section","text":"Through the Gui_Lens interface, researchers can download the entire analytical output in a single step via Download Portfolio (.zip):\n\nPLATE11.svg to PLATE60.svg: All 12 composite vector plates at 145 mm width stored directly in the archive root for immediate document insertion,\nREPORT.txt: The complete 7-stage interpreted scientific report,\nANALYTICS.xlsx: Raw regression coefficients, ANOVA tables, and candidate coordinates,\nPNGPLOTS/: High-resolution standalone raster plots (960 times 1080textpx)."},{"title":"DoECISORY.jl","page":"Overview","location":"#DoECISORY.jl","category":"section","text":"Design of Experiments with Computational Interactive Sequential Optimisation for Response Yield\n\nDoECISORY.jl is an open-source, registered Julia package and stateless Dash web application developed for classical and adaptive Design of Experiments (DoE), Response Surface Methodology (RSM), stoichiometric verification, and multi-objective optimisation. Executable across local workstations, cloud environments, the native Julia REPL, and reactive Pluto.jl notebooks, it integrates stoichiometric formulation balancing, multi-response statistical modelling, and interactive visual analytics within adaptive sequential workflows that contract and translate search spaces from screening to fine-tuning. Integrated first-order kinetic compensations correct for time-dependent reagent degradation: normalising pre-reaction preparation intervals, restoring end-of-synthesis (EOS) yields, and arbitrating reaction trade-offs via kinetic penalty profiling.\n\n"},{"title":"Core Capabilities","page":"Overview","location":"#Core-Capabilities","category":"section","text":"3-Factor Experimental Designs: Matrices for 3-factor formulation spaces, including Box-Behnken (BB15), Central Composite (CD17), Taguchi (TL09), and Fractional D-Optimal (DF14).\nInformation Optimality Metrics: Evaluation of D-, A-, G-, and I-efficiencies and design matrix condition numbers.\nStoichiometry and Mass Balance: Automated mass balance audits, excipient balancing, molar ratios, and concentration consistency checks (Lib_Mole).\nTemporal Kinetic Adjustments: Pre-reaction ingredient degradation normalisation (e.g. stock holding delay, radionuclide decay, or labile precursor degradation) and post-reaction analytical latency restoration back to reference state (e.g. End of Synthesis - EOS) via VISE_ApplyFRDecay_DDEF and MOLE_CalcRadioDecay_DDEF.\nMulti-Objective Optimisation and Kinetic Penalties (DCYP): Derringer-Suich desirability profiling with continuous and categorical criteria:\nDecay-Coupled Yield Penalty (DCYP): Universal kinetic trade-off arbitration penalising excessive reaction duration against exponential half-life decay (t_12, e.g. radionuclide decay or labile compound degradation).\nGaussian Neighbour-Weighting Regularisation: Smoothens multi-objective landscape ridges for stable metaheuristic and grid convergence.\nCandidate Pool Formulation: Generates a tiered portfolio of viable formulations rather than a single point:\nAbsolute Leaders (TOP-XX): Highest unconstrained composite desirability.\nInput Minimisation (INP-<Factor>): Green chemistry and cost-saving formulations achieving ge 90 desirability with minimal reagent consumption, reduced heating load, or shortest residence time.\nOutput Specialisation (OUT-<Response>): Solutions prioritising critical quality attributes like minimal impurity, high purity, or specific activity.\nSequential Interphase Transfer (ACTA and ASTM): Bridges successive experimental phases through:\nACTA (Adaptive Contraction & Translation Algorithm): Evaluates relative position (p_i) to contract search boundaries (c) around the leader or translate them (delta) upon boundary contact.\nASTM (Affine Space Transformation Model): Applies affine scaling (x_textnew = alpha cdot x_textold + beta) and absolute physical clamping.\nStatistical Diagnostics: Seven-stage scientific analysis report featuring OLS linear and quadratic regression, ANOVA, Lack-of-Fit tests, cross-validated Q^2 (overfitting protection), and VIF multicollinearity screening.\nVisualisation: PlotlyJS 3D response surfaces, equipotential 2D contour maps with fixed-factor slicing, and IPKT interval shift charts (Lib_Arts).\nSpreadsheet Data Exchange (Excel): Multi-sheet protocol workbooks (.xlsx) with formatting standards, formula preservation, and zero data loss (Sys_Fast).\n\n"},{"title":"Execution Modes","page":"Overview","location":"#Execution-Modes","category":"section","text":"DoECISORY.jl provides two operational interfaces:"},{"title":"Graphical Interface (GUI)","page":"Overview","location":"#[Graphical-Interface-(GUI)](interface.md)","category":"section","text":"Users: Laboratory chemists, radiopharmacists, radiochemists, and formulation scientists.\nInterface: Browser-based interactive Dash platform (launch()).\nWorkflow: Graphical factor setup, automatic Excel workbook download, dropzone data ingestion, interactive 3D Plotly surfaces, and slider-controlled IPKT transitions without terminal coding."},{"title":"Scripting and Batch Pipeline","page":"Overview","location":"#[Scripting-and-Batch-Pipeline](scripting.md)","category":"section","text":"Users: Biostatisticians, data scientists, chemical engineers, and automation specialists.\nInterface: Julia REPL, reactive Pluto.jl notebooks, or automated batch scripts.\nWorkflow: Direct API dispatch, multi-threaded high-density grid evaluations (--threads=auto), and ahead-of-time sysimage compilation (tools/Compiler.jl).\n\n"},{"title":"Installation","page":"Overview","location":"#Installation","category":"section","text":"Install the package via the Julia package manager:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"DoECISORY\")\n\nOr clone the development branch directly from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/erensgol/DoECISORY.jl\")\n\n"},{"title":"Minimal Verification Example","page":"Overview","location":"#Minimal-Verification-Example","category":"section","text":"Synthesise a standard 15-point Box-Behnken design (:bb15), evaluate optimality, fit a quadratic response surface from experimental observations, predict the centre point, and apply an IPKT interphase adaptation:\n\nusing DoECISORY\n\nbegin\n# 1. Synthesise experimental design matrix\n  plan = design(:bb15;\n    factors = [\"Temperature\", \"Time\", \"Precursor\"],\n    levels  = [(60.0, 90.0, 120.0), (5.0, 15.0, 25.0), (15.0, 25.0, 35.0)]\n  )\n  println(\"D-Efficiency: \", round(plan.metrics[\"D\"] * 100.0, digits=2), \"%\")\n\n# 2. Analytical response observations (Isolated RCY, %)\n  X = plan.matrix\n  Y = [77.6, 45.2, 66.1, 69.9, 72.4, 77.1, 57.4, 73.8, 67.5, 74.2, 77.9, 51.1, 70.3, 63.4, 73.8]\n\n# 3. Fit quadratic response surface model\n  model = VISE_Regress_DDEF(X, Y, \"quadratic\"; InNames=plan.factors)\n  println(\"Model R²: \", round(model[\"R2\"], digits=3))\n\n# 4. Predict response at the centre point\n  pred = VISE_Predict_DDEF(model, [90.0, 15.0, 25.0])\n  println(\"Predicted Centre Value: \", round(pred[1], digits=2))\n\n# 5. Interphase Knowledge Transfer (IPKT = ACTA + ASTM)\n# Step A: ACTA domain contraction (c = 0.50 around centre 90.0)\n  acta_levels = FLOW_CalcACTA_DDEF(90.0, [60.0, 90.0, 120.0], 0.50, 0.0, 0.0)\n\n# Step B: ASTM affine scaling and boundary safety verification (α = 0.9, β = 9.0)\n  phase2_levels = [FLOW_ApplyASTM_DDEF(x, 0.9, 9.0) for x in acta_levels]\n  println(\"Adapted Phase 2 Levels (Temp): \", round.(phase2_levels, digits=2))\nend\n\n"},{"title":"Documentation Contents","page":"Overview","location":"#Documentation-Contents","category":"section","text":"Pages = [\n    \"methodology.md\",\n    \"interface.md\",\n    \"scripting.md\",\n    \"tutorial.md\",\n    \"statistics.md\",\n    \"visuals.md\",\n    \"api.md\",\n    \"citation.md\"\n]\nDepth = 2"}]
}
